 at most that amount, which is absorbed into the universal constants of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$.\\\\\\\\n   109\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\\\\\n   110\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   111\\\\\\\\tThe soundness properties of the low individual degree game are expressed in terms of the following class of measurements.\\\\\\\\n   112\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   113\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Low-degree polynomial measurements]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:ld-meas}\\\\\\\\n   114\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:polyfunc, def:polymeasurements, def:polynomials-degree}\\\\\\\\n   115\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ denote the set of POVM measurements indexed by $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$, the polynomial representatives of individual degree at most $d$ in each variable; this is the notion of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:polymeasurements}, and the argument order $(m,q,d)$ follows that definition (the paper orders the arguments as $(m,d,q)$). By Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:polynomials-degree}, evaluation identifies these representatives with the corresponding polynomial functions $g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, bijectively whenever $d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le q-1$; the parameter regimes of this chapter (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:introparams} fixes $d = 1$, and admissibility gives $q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 2$) always satisfy this. More generally, for an integer $k$ and tuples $m = (m_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m_k)$, $q = (q_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,q_k)$, $d = (d_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,d_k)$, let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$ denote the set of measurements $G = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{G_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ such that for each $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,k\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, the outcome index $g_i$ is a polynomial $g_i\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_{q_i}^{m_i} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_{q_i}$ of individual degree at most $d_i$.\\\\\\\\n   116\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   117\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   118\\\\\\\\tQuantum soundness of a version of the classical low-degree test for polynomials of low \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{total} degree was claimed by Natarajan and Vidick, building on an analysis of a three-prover version of the test by Vidick; there is a known gap in the soundness analysis of that test, and it is not used here. Instead, the construction relies on the low \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{individual} degree test, whose quantum soundness is proved in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} and generalized in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}. The statement below is the form most directly useful in what follows.\\\\\\\\n   119\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   120\\\\\\\\t% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.\\\\\\\\n   121\\\\\\\\t% We keep both the environment and the label verbatim for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\\\\\n   122\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{theorem}[Quantum soundness of the simultaneous classical low individual degree test]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:ld-soundness}\\\\\\\\n   123\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:projective-strategy-general, def:tensor-product-value, def:povm-distance, def:bracket}\\\\\\\\n   124\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThere exists a function\\\\\\\\n   125\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   126\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps, q, m, d, k) = a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,(dmk)^a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\n   127\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   128\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tfor universal constants $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ and $0 < b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 1$ such that the following holds. For all $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps > 0$ and every parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ as in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-game} (so $m, d, k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$, $q$ is an admissible field size, and $m$ divides $q$), and for all projective strategies $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi, A, B)$ that succeed with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$ in the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}}$, there exist measurements\\\\\\\\n   129\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   130\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tG^w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)\\\\\\\\n   131\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   132\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ton $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hilb_w$ for $w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, where by slight abuse of notation $m$, $q$, $d$ denote the constant tuples $(m,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m)$, $(q,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,q)$, $(d,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,d)$ of length $k$, such that\\\\\\\\n   133\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   134\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u}_{a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}\\\\\\\\n   135\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}}\\\\\\\\n   136\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tI_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}_{[(g_1(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k)]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   137\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tG^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}_{[(g_1(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k)]} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}\\\\\\\\n   138\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}}\\\\\\\\n   139\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tI_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u}_{a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   140\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tG^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}\\\\\\\\n   141\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}}\\\\\\\\n   142\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tI_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n   143\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   144\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps,q,m,d,k)$, all three approximations are with respect to the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$, and the bracket notation for post-processed outcomes is that of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:bracket}. The first two approximations hold on average over the uniform distribution on $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$; no question distribution is associated with the third.\\\\\\\\n   145\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{theorem}\\\\\\\\n   146\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   147\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   148\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma^q$ be the Reed--Solomon code of degree $d$: the set of all $(p(x_1),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,p(x_q))$ where $p\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ is a univariate polynomial of degree at most $d$ and $x_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,x_q$ enumerate $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$. We may assume $d < q$. In the opposite regime the statement is immediate: for $d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge q$ the prefactor gives $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,(dmk)^a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, q^{-b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge q^{a-b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$, using $m, k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$, while the quantity bounded in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-distance} never exceeds $2$ for families of POVM elements, so the three approximations hold for an arbitrary choice of $G^w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$. For $d < q$, evaluation is injective on polynomials of degree at most $d$ and a nonzero polynomial of degree at most $d$ vanishes at no more than $d$ of the $q$ points, so $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code$ is a linear $[q,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, d+1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, q-d]_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma$ code.\\\\\\\\n   149\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the\\\\\\\\n   150\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% intended value n = q the printed distance q-d+1 exceeds the true minimum distance\\\\\\\\n   151\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% q-d of the degree-d evaluation code, and the dimension claim d+1 requires d < q.\\\\\\\\n   152\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% Both points are corrected above; the regime d >= q, which the parameter tuple of\\\\\\\\n   153\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% Definition def:ld-game permits, is handled by the triviality argument. The\\\\\\\\n   154\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% boundary values d = 0 and k = 0 are excluded by the parameter domain of\\\\\\\\n   155\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% Definition def:ld-game, following the source's convention that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N denotes\\\\\\\\n   156\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% the positive integers.\\\\\\\\n   157\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source asserts a flat identity between the two tests\\\\\\\\n   158\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:446-454).\\\\\\\\n   159\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% After the zero-direction correction of Definition def:ld-win-predicate\\\\\\\\n   160\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (see rem:ld-win-zero-direction) the identity holds only away from\\\\\\\\n   161\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% zero-direction diagonal questions, so the import is scoped to the predicate\\\\\\\\n   162\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% analyzed in the external source and bridged by the probability estimate below,\\\\\\\\n   163\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% which is insensitive to the external test's acceptance convention on that event.\\\\\\\\n   164\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, agrees with the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$ on every question pair whose diagonal-line direction is nonzero: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. On diagonal line-versus-point questions with direction $v' = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$, the win predicate of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} resolves an ambiguity of the source (Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}), and the resolution is not required to match the acceptance convention of the subcube commutation test on this event: the event has probability at most $2/(mq)$ under the question distribution, since the direction $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ vanishes with probability $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac1m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{j=1}^m q^{-j} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{m(q-1)} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{2}{mq}$ over uniform $(s,v)$, so the success probabilities of any fixed strategy in the two games differ by at most $2/(mq)$. A projective strategy that succeeds in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$ therefore succeeds in the tensor-code test with probability at least $1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps - 2/(mq)$. Theorem~4.7 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}$) and applied with the success deficit $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps + 2/(mq)$ in place of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, then yields the present statement for $k = 1$: since $0 < b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 1$ and $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ give $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps + 2/(mq))^b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b + 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,q^{-b}$, the additional error is absorbed into $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ by enlarging the universal constant $a$, using $dmk \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.\\\\\\\\n   165\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   166\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   167\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Relation to the low individual degree test of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:test}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:ld-soundness-provider}\\\\\\\\n   168\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tTheorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is imported: the proof above reduces it to Theorem~4.7 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$, followed by the extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The result of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} that underlies it is proved here as Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}: for every projective strategy passing the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, that theorem produces measurements in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ satisfying consistency relations of the same shape as the three approximations of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is not a consequence of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal} alone: a derivation from it would require, in addition, an equivalence between the typed game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{(q,m,d,1)}$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:test} --- matching the two question distributions, the two win conditions, and the two error functions --- together with the extension from $k = 1$ to general $k$ by the reduction of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} cited above. Neither implication is proved in this chapter, and the proof of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} given above does not invoke Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}.\\\\\\\\n   169\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\\\\\n   170\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   171\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\section{The Magic Square game}\\\\\\\\n   172\\\\\\\\t% Paper label sec:ms kept for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\\\\\n   173\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:ms}\\\\\\\\n   174\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   175\\\\\\\\tWe recall the Magic Square game of Mermin and Peres. It is a simple self-test for two EPR pairs, and it allows one to test that a pair of binary observables \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{anticommutes}; both properties are used in the Pauli basis test. Here it is presented as a binary constraint satisfaction game. Throughout this section and the next, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ denote the single-qubit Pauli observables, with associated two-outcome projective measurements $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^W_b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2}$ for $W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\\\\\n   176\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   177\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[The Magic Square game]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:ms-game}\\\\\\\\n   178\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:game, def:graph-distribution}\\\\\\\\n   179\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe Magic Square game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{MS}}$ is the following two-player game. There are $9$ variables $x_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,x_9$ taking values in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2$, arranged in a $3 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times 3$ grid: the cell in row $r$ and column $c$ contains the variable $x_{3(r-1)+c}$. There are $6$ linear equations over these variables: for each of the three rows and each of the first two columns, the sum of the three variables in it equals $0$; for the third column, the sum of the three variables in it equals $1$.\\\\\\\\n   180\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-01.jsonl:212:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_107\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg -n 'source (states|prints|asserts|claims)|The source|paper (states|prints|asserts|claims)|does not|fails|false|defect|gap|typo|slip|correction' blueprint/src/chapter/ch1[1-6]_qpbt_*.tex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:22:% Local correction of the source (lines 1419 and 1492 of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:28:\\\\\\\\tThe source states the constructions of this chapter in terms of its boolean decoding map (the case $H = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:decoding-map}) while describing that map as returning the string of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{all} evaluations of $g$ on the hypercube. The proofs require the latter reading, $H = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, which is adopted throughout this chapter: the linearity of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}$ and the identity $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for all $h \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^{M}$, both of which fail for the boolean map, are used in the symmetry step of the proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} and in the conjugation identity \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}. The source further asserts the identity $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)$ for every $g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q)$. Since the left-hand side equals $g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}(u)$, the identity holds exactly when $g$ is an encoding, and fails otherwise: for $m = 1$, $d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 2$, and $q > 2$, the polynomial $g(x) = x^2$ has $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_1(u) = u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq u^2$. Accordingly, the proofs below invoke the identity only for encodings; in the proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} the contribution of the remaining outcomes is controlled by the bound \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} on the mass that the measurement of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} places on polynomials that are not encodings, while in the conjugation identity \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the quantity $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:94:% Local correction of the source (lines 1451-1456 of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:95:% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex): the source asserts commutation\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:98:\\\\\\\\tFor $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq j'$ the source asserts that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{X}^j(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u})$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v})$ commute for all $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^M$. Since $e_j e_{j'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$ and the bilinear form $(a,b) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mapsto \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr(ab)$ on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ is nondegenerate, there exist $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v}$ with $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr(e_j e_{j'} (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v})) = 1$, and for these \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} yields anticommutation instead. By self-duality of the basis $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{e_j\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, the dichotomy asserted by the source --- commutation for $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq j'$, and the phase $(-1)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr((e_j\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (e_j\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v}))}$ for $j = j'$ --- agrees with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} whenever $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, in particular for all $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}^M$. The relations \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} are not used in the remainder of the chapter.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:108:\\\\\\\\t% The paper's statement also quantifies item 1 over j, but j does not occur\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:264:\\\\\\\\tFor the first item, the self-consistency of the observables $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{W}^j$ (Item~2 of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}) is equivalent, since these observables are Hermitian involutions (Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:tildew-product-form}), to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^M} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hat\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{W}^j(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{W}^j(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hat\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S/2$; setting $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} = V_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot V_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hat\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and using the exact conjugation identities of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:v-swap-conjugation}, this becomes $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} H_W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S/2$ for $W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, where $H_W = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^W(e_j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^W(e_j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl( \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{s \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^W(s) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^W(s) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M}$ by the tensor factorization of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^W(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u})$ and the invariance of the uniform average over $s$ under $s \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mapsto e_j s$. By Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:cancellation}, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{s,s'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^X(s)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^Z(s') \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^X(s)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^Z(s') = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ on each pair of qudits, whence $H_X H_Z = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M}$. The bounds $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id - H_W)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} H_W^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$, combined through the triangle inequality $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| H_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} - H_Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$ (see Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}), give $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M})_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}''\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - 4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:266:\\\\\\\\t% without ensuring that it is nonzero; see Remark rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:270:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Two steps in the source proof of the extraction lemma]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:271:\\\\\\\\tIn the source proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, the triangle inequality gives $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| H_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} - H_Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id - H_X)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id - H_Z)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$, so that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| H_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} - H_Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$; the source instead asserts the bound $2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$ for the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{squared} norm, which holds only when $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 1/2$, and consequently carries the term $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$ into an overlap bound of $1 - 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$. The proof above uses the corrected bound $4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$, which gives the overlap bound $1 - 4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ and leaves the final bound $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}} = O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ unchanged. The source proof also normalizes the vector $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M})_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}''\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta}$ without ensuring that it is nonzero; the case distinction on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ in the proof above supplies this, the conclusions of the lemma being trivial when $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ exceeds a universal constant.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:53:\\\\\\\\tThe symmetrization argument of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} below invokes Naimark dilation in the form of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:naimark} (stated and proved in Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:projective}): a bipartite state together with two sub-measurement families can be replaced, without changing any of the correlations $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^y_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$, by a product-extended state together with projective sub-measurements on the enlarged spaces. When the input families are complete measurements, the dilated projective sub-measurements can be completed by assigning the residual projector of each family to one fixed outcome; the resulting projective measurements reproduce the same correlations on the extended state, since the correlation-preservation identity forces the extended state to lie in the range of the total projector of each dilated family. This completed form, with genuinely projective measurements, is the one used in Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat}; for the proof the source refers to Theorem 5.1 of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}, which is stated for complete measurements. The source follows its statement of the dilation theorem with a sentence announcing an ``orthogonalization lemma'' from the literature that is never actually stated there; nothing in this chapter depends on it.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:95:% The source labels this definition def:projective-strategy. That label is already carried\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:118:% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:94-103) concludes with a\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:139:\\\\\\\\tThe source states the conclusion of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} in the stronger form: there exists a symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$. Its proof, reproduced above, produces, for each $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps' > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, such a strategy of value at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps'$, and the stronger form does not follow: the dilated dimension $d$ may grow as $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\downarrow \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, and the supremum defining $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\operatorname{val}^*(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:tensor-product-value} need not be attained by any strategy. The statement above therefore records the approximate conclusion, which is what the proof establishes; the stronger form would require an additional attainment or compactness argument.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:264:\\\\\\\\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:347-361) ends with\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:357:% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:465-495) leaves the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:372:\\\\\\\\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:465-495) prints the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:387:\\\\\\\\tThe source proof is a citation: the argument is identical to the proof of Fact 4.34 in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, with the only modification being the insertion of the dependence on the question $x$ for all measurements considered. In outline, the consistency hypotheses \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-1} allow one to insert the projective factors $G^{i,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}$ next to $A^x$ one index at a time, and the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G_i$ controls the events on which two different outcomes collide at the sampled point $y$. Each of the $k$ insertions costs $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps)^{1/2}$ by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, producing the sandwich $C^x$ with total error $k(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps)^{1/2}$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:391:\\\\\\\\tAs printed in the source, the sandwich in Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich} pairs the $i$-th measurement with the outcome $g_{k+1-i}$: the outermost factor $G^{k,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}$ carries the outcome $g_1$ and the innermost factor $G^{1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}$ carries $g_k$. Since the measurement $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{G^{i,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}_g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ is indexed by $g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G_i$, the operator $G^{k,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}_{g_1}$ is undefined whenever $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G_1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G_k$, so the printed pairing is a typographical slip. The statement above instead uses the pairing $G^{k,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}_{g_k} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdots G^{1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}_{g_1} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdots G^{k,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,x}_{g_k}$ of Fact 4.34 in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, from which the lemma is imported. The source also leaves the case $k = 0$ implicit; with the empty-product convention recorded in the statement, both sides of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-2} are then the identity on the single empty-tuple outcome and the conclusion holds with error zero.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:539:% normalization 1/(2m-k) below is undefined. The source\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:422:\\\\\\\\t% The source states delta_combine(eps,m,d,q) = poly(m^2 eps, md/q)\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:426:\\\\\\\\t% rem:qld-4-13-source-defects. The divisibility hypothesis is required\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:548:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source phrases the next property as: the marginal over\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:552:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% over pairs of lines, so the phrase does not type-check as\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:627:\\\\\\\\t% The source proof describes w as \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a direction vector whose first\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:718:\\\\\\\\tThe source proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:784:\\\\\\\\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity. The source right-hand side\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:785:\\\\\\\\t% carries a typographical superscript \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" in place of the point \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\";\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:843:\\\\\\\\tthan for its deficit; see Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}).\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:952:\\\\\\\\tit. (The source states a second bound for the same quantity, whose\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:954:\\\\\\\\tdistribution; see Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}.) Now\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:970:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:971:\\\\\\\\tThe source proof in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar} states two different bounds for\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:998:\\\\\\\\ttend to $0$ while the term $m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^{1/4} = 1$ does not, so the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:999:\\\\\\\\tderivation does not support the claimed form. Even granting the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1142:% This remark records a defect of the source argument; it is not a statement\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1157:\\\\\\\\twritten. This hypothesis does not follow from admissibility of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1165:\\\\\\\\t$2m+2 = 4$ divides every admissible $q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 4$). The source proof in\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1168:\\\\\\\\tparameters with $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 2$ the hypothesis fails, the statement of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:19:\\\\\\\\t% The source fixes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N to be the set of positive integers\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:97:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source phrases both clauses with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" t such that x = u_0 + tv; that phrase\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:98:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% does not determine t on diagonal questions with direction v' = 0, which the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:100:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% quantification over t used here is the local correction; see the remark below.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:108:\\\\\\\\tThe source states the two line-versus-point clauses with ``the'' $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ such that $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t v$. When the direction is nonzero and $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w}$ lies on the line, exactly one such $t$ exists and the formulation above agrees with the source. But zero directions occur: Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:line} explicitly permits them, and the question distribution of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} produces $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}$ questions with $v' = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$ with positive probability, namely whenever the last $m - i + 1$ coordinates of $v$ vanish. In that case $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0, 0) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{u_0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, every $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ satisfies $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$ when $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0$, and the source clause does not determine acceptance for a nonconstant answer polynomial. The universal quantification over $t$ adopted in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} makes the predicate a well-defined function of its arguments; it accepts vacuously when no such $t$ exists, a case the question distribution never produces. Completeness is unaffected: the restriction of a polynomial $g$ to the singleton line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,0)$, expressed through the parameterization $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mapsto u_0 + t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot 0$, is the constant polynomial with value $g(u_0)$, which passes the check for every $t$. Soundness is likewise unaffected: zero directions occur with probability at most $2/(mq)$, and the proof of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} shows that any acceptance convention on this event changes success probabilities by at most that amount, which is absorbed into the universal constants of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:118:Quantum soundness of a version of the classical low-degree test for polynomials of low \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{total} degree was claimed by Natarajan and Vidick, building on an analysis of a three-prover version of the test by Vidick; there is a known gap in the soundness analysis of that test, and it is not used here. Instead, the construction relies on the low \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{individual} degree test, whose quantum soundness is proved in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} and generalized in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}. The statement below is the form most directly useful in what follows.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:120:% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:149:\\\\\\\\t% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:157:\\\\\\\\t% The source asserts a flat identity between the two tests\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:159:\\\\\\\\t% After the zero-direction correction of Definition def:ld-win-predicate\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:168:\\\\\\\\tTheorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is imported: the proof above reduces it to Theorem~4.7 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$, followed by the extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The result of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} that underlies it is proved here as Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}: for every projective strategy passing the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, that theorem produces measurements in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ satisfying consistency relations of the same shape as the three approximations of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is not a consequence of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal} alone: a derivation from it would require, in addition, an equivalence between the typed game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{(q,m,d,1)}$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:test} --- matching the two question distributions, the two win conditions, and the two error functions --- together with the extension from $k = 1$ to general $k$ by the reduction of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} cited above. Neither implication is proved in this chapter, and the proof of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} given above does not invoke Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:260:\\\\\\\\t% The source introduces q, m, d in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N the positive integers\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:384:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The paper here has the typo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"phi_alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot phi_B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; both maps are the isometries above.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:470:\\\\\\\\tThe source proof bounds the third term of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}}$ by asserting $2^{-bmd} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le q^{-b}$ ``because $k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le m$'', comparing the field exponent $k = c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lceil \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rceil + 1$ with $m = 2^j$. The inequality $k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le m$ does not follow from Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:introparams}: if the universal constants of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:pauli} were $a = 1$ and $b = 1/2$, then $c = 4$, and $R = 5$ gives $k = 9$ and $m = 8$. The proof above instead bounds $2^{-bm} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le R^{-b}$ directly from $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge (c/2) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R$, which yields the same conclusion after enlarging the universal constant $a'$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:8:% The source restates thm:pauli verbatim at the head of the appendix under the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:74:\\\\\\\\t$f$ does not evaluate at $u$, and for a family $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{N_f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ indexed by\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:83:\\\\\\\\tThe source refers to the elements of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell)$ as polynomials\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:139:\\\\\\\\t% The source states this result in a `fact' environment; fact environments\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:144:\\\\\\\\t% the source states those bounds without the hypothesis m, d >= 1, under\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:220:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Source correction: the anticommuting probability]\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:221:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:omega-anticomm-prob-correction}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:314:\\\\\\\\t% The source ends this display with the exact bound |A| * delta; under the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:522:\\\\\\\\t% The source labels this lemma `len:qld-win-implications-obs'; the prefix\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_obs