\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_108\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg -n 'Assume that|assuming that|provided that|additional hypothesis|extra hypothesis|hypothesis added|Hypothesis added|Scope restriction|vacuous|not established|does not establish|conditional' blueprint/src/chapter/ch1[1-6]_qpbt_*.tex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:109:\\\\\\\\t% in item 1; the vacuous quantifier is dropped here.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:431:\\\\\\\\tsuch that the following holds. Assume that $2m+2$ divides $q$. For\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:535:\\\\\\\\t% Hypothesis added: the line-point distribution over F_q^{2m+2} is built\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:541:\\\\\\\\tAssume that $2m+2$ divides $q$. Then there exists a distribution $D$\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:571:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tdistributed on the line conditionally on the line: within the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:580:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tabout the joint conditional law of the pair $(u_X, u_Z)$.)\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:712:\\\\\\\\tuniform on the line conditionally on the line. The equivalent product\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:713:\\\\\\\\tformulations follow from the same conditional uniformity.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:990:\\\\\\\\tsource is not established there. The proof above therefore uses only\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1022:\\\\\\\\t% Hypothesis added: the proof instantiates the classical low individual\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1031:\\\\\\\\tholds. Assume that $2m+2$ divides $q$. For\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1169:\\\\\\\\tLemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} is vacuous, and the conclusion of that lemma\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1170:\\\\\\\\tis not established for such parameters, here or in the source; a\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:8:Throughout, the two players are denoted $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B$; for $w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ we write $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w$ for the other player. Games, strategies, values, conditionally linear (CL) functions and distributions, typed distributions sampled from the edges of a graph, and the state-dependent approximation $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ between families of measurement operators are as in Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-games}; the algebraic material on lines, canonical linear maps, the low-degree encoding, and generalized Pauli observables is from Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra}.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:108:\\\\\\\\tThe source states the two line-versus-point clauses with ``the'' $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ such that $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t v$. When the direction is nonzero and $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w}$ lies on the line, exactly one such $t$ exists and the formulation above agrees with the source. But zero directions occur: Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:line} explicitly permits them, and the question distribution of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} produces $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}$ questions with $v' = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$ with positive probability, namely whenever the last $m - i + 1$ coordinates of $v$ vanish. In that case $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0, 0) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{u_0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, every $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ satisfies $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$ when $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0$, and the source clause does not determine acceptance for a nonconstant answer polynomial. The universal quantification over $t$ adopted in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} makes the predicate a well-defined function of its arguments; it accepts vacuously when no such $t$ exists, a case the question distribution never produces. Completeness is unaffected: the restriction of a polynomial $g$ to the singleton line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,0)$, expressed through the parameterization $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mapsto u_0 + t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot 0$, is the constant polynomial with value $g(u_0)$, which passes the check for every $t$. Soundness is likewise unaffected: zero directions occur with probability at most $2/(mq)$, and the proof of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} shows that any acceptance convention on this event changes success probabilities by at most that amount, which is absorbed into the universal constants of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:146:\\\\\\\\t% unconditional constant bounds are what the proof of the lemma labelled\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:241:\\\\\\\\tsupplies the unconditional constant lower bounds used in the proof of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:419:\\\\\\\\tAssume that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{S}$ succeeds in $G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:500:\\\\\\\\titems~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-comm} and~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-comm-cons} accept vacuously\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:502:\\\\\\\\titems~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-ms} and~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-ms-cons} accept vacuously\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:589:\\\\\\\\tconditional averages.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:1:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chapter{Games, strategies, and conditionally linear distributions}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:6:This chapter records the vocabulary in which the quantum Pauli basis test and its analysis are phrased: two-player one-round games and tensor product strategies, the consistency and state-dependent distance calculus for families of measurements, and conditionally linear functions and distributions, the class of question distributions used by the tests of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-test}. The presentation follows \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}; the distance calculus is imported there from \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:35:% on 2026-08-30; decidable-equality instance arguments encode no extra hypothesis over\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:437:Conditionally linear functions specify the question distributions of the games considered in the following chapters in a form that can be ``introspected''. Intuitively, a conditionally linear function takes as input an element $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in V = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F^n$ and applies linear maps $L_j$ sequentially to $x^{V_j}$, where $V_1, V_2, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dots$ is a sequence of complementary register subspaces such that both the linear map $L_j$ and the subspace $V_j$, for $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 2$, may depend on the values taken by the previous linear maps $L_1(x^{V_1})$, $L_2(x^{V_2})$, and so on.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:447:\\\\\\\\tLet $V$ be $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F^n$ for some $n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 0$. For all integers $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 0$ the collection of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$-level conditionally linear functions} (implicitly, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{on $V$}) is defined inductively as follows.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:449:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item There is a single $0$-level conditionally linear function, which is the $0$ function on $V$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:450:\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ and suppose the collection of $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell-1)$-level conditionally linear functions has been defined. The collection of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$-level conditionally linear functions on $V$ consists of all functions $L$ on $V$ that can be expressed in the following form: there exist complementary register subspaces $V_1$ and $V_{>1}$ of $V$, a linear function $L_1$ on $V_1$, and, for all $v \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in L_1(V_1)$, an $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell-1)$-level conditionally linear function $L_{>1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,v}$ on $V_{>1}$, such that for all $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in V$,\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:457:We abbreviate ``conditionally linear'' as \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{CL}. Operationally, an $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$-level CL function first applies $L_1$ to $x^{V_1}$; conditioned on the value $x^{L_1}$ it selects a register subspace $V_2$ and a linear map $L_2$ and applies $L_2$ to $x^{V_2}$; and so on, finally outputting $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{k=1}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell} x^{L_k}$. Every $1$-level CL function is linear, and conversely every linear map on $V$ is a $1$-level CL function. Moreover, for every $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ the collection of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$-level CL functions contains the collection of $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell-1)$-level CL functions.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:461:\\\\\\\\tLet $L, R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon V \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to V$ be conditionally linear functions. The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{conditionally linear distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L,R}$ corresponding to $(L,R)$} is the distribution over pairs $(L(x), R(x)) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in V \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times V$ for $x$ drawn uniformly at random from $V$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:466:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Structure of conditionally linear functions]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:cl-kth}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:490:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Concatenation of conditionally linear functions]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:cl-concat}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:496:\\\\\\\\tis a $(k+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell)$-level conditionally linear function on $U \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\oplus V$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:503:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Direct sums of conditionally linear functions]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:cl-func-prod}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:505:\\\\\\\\tLet $V^{(1)}, V^{(2)}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dots, V^{(m)}$ be register subspaces of $V$ such that $V = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigoplus_{j=1}^m V^{(j)}$. Suppose that, for each $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1, 2, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dots, m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, $L^{(j)}$ is an $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell_j$-level conditionally linear function on $V^{(j)}$. Then the direct sum $L = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigoplus_{j=1}^m L^{(j)}$, defined by\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:513:\\\\\\\\tSince an $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$-level CL function is also $k$-level conditionally linear for all $k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$, it suffices to prove the claim when $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell_j = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$ for all $j$. Proceed by induction on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$. For $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell = 1$ the functions $L^{(j)}$ are linear and the direct sum of linear maps is linear. For the inductive step, decompose each $L^{(j)}$ into its first-level linear part $L^{(j)}_1$ on $V^{(j)}_1$ and its family of $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell-1)$-level remainders $L^{(j)}_{>1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,v_j}$ on $V^{(j)}_{>1}$. The direct sums of the first-level parts define a linear map on $V_1 = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigoplus_j V^{(j)}_1$, and, for each prefix value $v = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_j v_j$, the direct sums of the remainders are $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell-1)$-level conditionally linear on $V_{>1} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigoplus_j V^{(j)}_{>1}$ by the induction hypothesis. This exhibits $L$ in the form required by Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:cl-func}.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:518:\\\\\\\\tFor each $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dots, m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ let $L^{(i)}, R^{(i)} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon V^{(i)} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to V^{(i)}$ be $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell_i$-level conditionally linear functions, and let $L, R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon V \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to V$ be the direct sums $L = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigoplus_i L^{(i)}$ and $R = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigoplus_i R^{(i)}$, as defined in Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:cl-func-prod}. Then the conditionally linear distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L,R}$ is the product distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\prod_{i=1}^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L^{(i)},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,R^{(i)}}$ over $V \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times V$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:524:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\section{Typed conditionally linear distributions}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:533:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Typed conditionally linear functions]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:typed-cl-functions}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:535:\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T$ be a finite set (the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{type set}) and let $V$ be $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F^n$ for some integer $n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 0$. A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T$-typed family of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$-level conditionally linear functions} (implicitly, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{on $V$}) is a collection $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{L_t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T}$ such that, for each $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T$, $L_t$ is an $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$-level conditionally linear function on $V$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:556:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Typed conditionally linear distributions]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:typed-cl-distributions}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:558:\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T$ be a type set and let $L = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{L_u\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T}$ and $R = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{R_v\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{v \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T}$ be $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T$-typed families of conditionally linear functions on $V$. Let $G = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T, E)$ be a graph with vertex set $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T$ and nonempty edge set $E$, so that the graph distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_G$ of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:graph-distribution} is defined. The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T, G)$-typed conditionally linear distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu^G_{L,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,R}$ corresponding to $(L, R)$} is the distribution over pairs $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl( (u, x), (v, y) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)$, where the type pair $(u,v)$ is drawn from the graph distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_G$ and the content pair $(x,y)$ is drawn from $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L_u,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,R_v}$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:6:machinery over finite fields (which underlies the conditionally linear question\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:455:\\\\\\\\t$i = 0$ the condition is vacuous, so every line is diagonal).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-01.jsonl:223:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_114\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex | sed -n '430,471p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex | sed -n '1,80p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex | sed -n '420,475p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex | sed -n '1450,1495p'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"     1\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chapter{The Pauli basis test}\\\\\\\\n     2\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{chap:qpbt-test}\\\\\\\\n     3\\\\\\\\t% Paper label sec:pauli-verifier kept for cross-reference fidelity; it resolves to this chapter.\\\\\\\\n     4\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:pauli-verifier}\\\\\\\\n     5\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n     6\\\\\\\\tThis chapter defines the two testing primitives underlying the question-reduction step: the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{classical low individual degree game}, in its simultaneous form, and the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{Pauli basis test}, a nonlocal game built from the classical game together with the Magic Square game. We state the quantum soundness of the classical game (Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, imported; see Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider}), the rigidity of the Magic Square game (Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:ms-rigidity}, imported), completeness of the Pauli basis test (Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:pauli-completeness}), and the main soundness theorem for the Pauli basis test (Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:pauli}), whose proof occupies Chapters~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-observables}--\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-extraction} and is completed in Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-extraction}. The chapter closes with the qubit form of the soundness statement (Corollary~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{cor:pauli-binary}) and the canonical parameter choice with its soundness bound (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:introparams}, Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:delta-bound}).\\\\\\\\n     7\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n     8\\\\\\\\tThroughout, the two players are denoted $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B$; for $w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ we write $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w$ for the other player. Games, strategies, values, conditionally linear (CL) functions and distributions, typed distributions sampled from the edges of a graph, and the state-dependent approximation $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ between families of measurement operators are as in Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-games}; the algebraic material on lines, canonical linear maps, the low-degree encoding, and generalized Pauli observables is from Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra}.\\\\\\\\n     9\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    10\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\section{The classical low individual degree game}\\\\\\\\n    11\\\\\\\\t% Paper labels sec:ld-verifier and sec:ld-game kept for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\\\\\n    12\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:ld-verifier}\\\\\\\\n    13\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:ld-game}\\\\\\\\n    14\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    15\\\\\\\\tThe classical low individual degree game is a two-player game based on the low individual degree variant of the multilinearity test of Babai, Fortnow, and Lund. In the basic version, one player (the ``points player'') receives a uniformly random point $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ and answers with a value $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, while the other player (the ``lines player'') receives a random line through $u$ --- either axis-parallel or diagonal --- and answers with a univariate polynomial describing the restriction of a global function to that line. If the players share a polynomial $g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ of individual degree at most $d$ and answer with $g(u)$ and with the restriction $g|_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$, they win with probability $1$; the soundness theorem below states that the converse approximately holds. In the simultaneous version, the players' answers are $k$-tuples, checked coordinatewise against a $k$-tuple of low individual degree polynomials.\\\\\\\\n    16\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    17\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[The classical low individual degree game: parameters and registers]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:ld-game}\\\\\\\\n    18\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:game, def:admissible-size}\\\\\\\\n    19\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source fixes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N to be the set of positive integers\\\\\\\\n    20\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (references/qpbt-paper/04_preliminaries.tex:6) and takes m, d, ldc in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N\\\\\\\\n    21\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:33-35),\\\\\\\\n    22\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% so the positivity below is the source's implicit domain, stated explicitly.\\\\\\\\n    23\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% It is load-bearing: at d = 0 or k = 0 the prefactor a(dmk)^a of delta_ld in\\\\\\\\n    24\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% lem:ld-soundness vanishes, and at d = 0 the resulting exact conclusions fail\\\\\\\\n    25\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (e.g. at (q,m,d,k) = (2,1,0,1) with eps = 1). The case k = 0, though it would\\\\\\\\n    26\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% hold trivially, is likewise outside the source's domain and is omitted.\\\\\\\\n    27\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ is parametrized by a tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$, where $m, d, k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ are integers, $q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N$ is an admissible field size (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:admissible-size}), and $m$ divides $q$. We write $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ to emphasize the dependence on the parameter tuple. The type set of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ is\\\\\\\\n    28\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n    29\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n    30\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n    31\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tand the distribution over type pairs $(t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}, t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B})$ is uniform over $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$. The ambient space is the direct sum $V = V_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\oplus V_I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\oplus V_V$ of three register subspaces: the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{point register} $V_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cong \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$, the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{coordinate register} $V_I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cong \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, and the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{direction register} $V_V \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cong \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$. Elements of $V$ are identified with triples $(u,s,v) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$. The question distribution and the win predicate of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ are specified in Definitions~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} and~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} below.\\\\\\\\n    32\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n    33\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    34\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Question distribution of the classical low individual degree game]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:ld-question-distribution}\\\\\\\\n    35\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-game, def:cl-func, def:cl-dist, lem:cl-concat, def:cl-canonical, def:line-representative}\\\\\\\\n    36\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ be as in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-game}. Define $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}}$ to be the $1$-level CL function on $V$ that projects onto $V_X$: for every $(u,s,v) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in V$,\\\\\\\\n    37\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\n    38\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:cl-ptf}\\\\\\\\n    39\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tL_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}}(u,s,v) = (u,0,0)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;.\\\\\\\\n    40\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n    41\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFor $s \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$ be the unique integer such that\\\\\\\\n    42\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\n    43\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:chi-func}\\\\\\\\n    44\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ts = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)-1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{q}{m} + r\\\\\\\\n    45\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n    46\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tfor some $0 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le r < q/m$, where $s$ is interpreted as an integer in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,q-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ under the identification of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ with binary strings fixed in Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra}. Since $m$ divides $q$, when $s$ is uniformly distributed on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ the index $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$ is uniformly distributed on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$. Define $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}$ by\\\\\\\\n    47\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\n    48\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:cl-alnf}\\\\\\\\n    49\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tL_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}(u,s,v) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl(L^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Ln}}_{e_i}(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, s,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, 0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\quad\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\text{for all } (u,s,v) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in V\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n    50\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n    51\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$ and $L^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Ln}}_v$ denotes the canonical linear map with one-dimensional kernel spanned by $v$, used to compute canonical representatives of lines (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:line-representative}). The function $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}$ is the concatenation (in the sense of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:cl-concat}) of the $1$-level CL function projecting $V_I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\oplus V_V$ onto $V_I$ with the family of $1$-level CL functions $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{L^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Ln}}_{e_i}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ on $V_X$ indexed by $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$; thus $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}$ is a $2$-level CL function. Define $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}$ by\\\\\\\\n    52\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\n    53\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:cl-dlnf}\\\\\\\\n    54\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tL_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}(u,s,v) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl(L^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Ln}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)}(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, s,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n    55\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n    56\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}$ zeroes out the first $i-1$ coordinates of its input. The function $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}$ is the concatenation of (i) the identity on $V_I$, (ii) for each $i$ the $1$-level CL function $v \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mapsto \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ on $V_V$, and (iii) for each pair $(s,v') \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in V_I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\oplus V_V$ the $1$-level CL function $L^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Ln}}_{v'}$ on $V_X$; thus $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}$ is a $3$-level CL function.\\\\\\\\n    57\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    58\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe question distribution of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ is the typed CL distribution obtained as follows: sample a type pair $(t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}, t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B})$ uniformly from $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$, sample $z = (u,s,v)$ uniformly from $V$, and send each player $w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ the question $(t_w, L_{t_w}(z))$.\\\\\\\\n    59\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n    60\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    61\\\\\\\\tThe next two lemmas show that the CL functions $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}}$, $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}$, $L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}$ give rise to the axis-parallel and diagonal line-versus-point distributions.\\\\\\\\n    62\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    63\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:alnf}\\\\\\\\n    64\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:line}\\\\\\\\n    65\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tConsider the pair $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl((u_0,s,0),(u,0,0)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)$ from the CL distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}, L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}}}$, and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0, e_i)$ where $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$. Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$, and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$ is a line that goes through $u$ and is parallel to a uniformly random axis $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\\\\\n    66\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n    67\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n    68\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:line-representative}\\\\\\\\n    69\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tSince $s$ is uniformly random in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ and $m$ divides $q$, the index $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$ is uniformly random in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, so $(u_0,s)$ specifies a uniformly random axis-parallel line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,e_i)$. Since $u_0 = L^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Ln}}_{e_i}(u)$ is the canonical representative of the line through $u$ in direction $e_i$, we have $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,e_i)$.\\\\\\\\n    70\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n    71\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    72\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:dlnf}\\\\\\\\n    73\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:line}\\\\\\\\n    74\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tConsider the pair $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl((u_0,s,v),(u,0,0)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)$ from the CL distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}, L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}}}$, and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0, v)$ where $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$. Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$, and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell$ is a random diagonal line that goes through $u$ and shares its first $i-1$ coordinates with $u$, for uniformly random $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\\\\\n    75\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n    76\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n    77\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{lem:alnf}\\\\\\\\n    78\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe proof is identical to that of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:alnf}: uniformity of $s$ makes $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$ uniform, the direction component of the sampled tuple is $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ for uniform $v$ (so its first $i-1$ coordinates vanish), and $u_0 = L^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Ln}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)}(u)$ places $u$ on the line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v))$.\\\\\\\\n    79\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n    80\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    81\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Line-point distributions]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:line-point-dist}\\\\\\\\n    82\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, lem:alnf, lem:dlnf}\\\\\\\\n    83\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{axis line-point distribution} $D_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}$ is the distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}, L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}}}$ over pairs $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell = (u_0,e_i),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u)$, where we omit the $0$ components for simplicity. The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{diagonal line-point distribution} $D_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}$ is the distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu_{L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}, L_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}}}$ over pairs $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell = (u_0,s,v),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u)$. The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{line-point distribution} $D_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Line}}$ is the equal mixture of the two: with probability $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac12$ output a sample from $D_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}}$, and with probability $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac12$ output a sample from $D_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}}$.\\\\\\\\n    84\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n    85\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    86\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Win predicate of the classical low individual degree game]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:ld-win-predicate}\\\\\\\\n    87\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:line, def:polyfunc}\\\\\\\\n    88\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFix $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$. Answers in the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ take the following forms, depending on the question type; answers not of the prescribed form are rejected.\\\\\\\\n    89\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{itemize}\\\\\\\\n    90\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item Type $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}$, question content $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$: the answer is an element of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^k$.\\\\\\\\n    91\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item Type $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}$, question content $(u_0,s) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, specifying the axis-parallel line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,e_i)$ with $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$: the answer is a $k$-tuple $(f_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,f_k)$ of univariate polynomials $f_j\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ of degree at most $d$, each given by its $d+1$ coefficients.\\\\\\\\n    92\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item Type $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}$, question content $(u_0,s,v) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$, specifying the diagonal line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,v')$ with $v' = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ and $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$: the answer is a $k$-tuple of univariate polynomials of degree at most $md$.\\\\\\\\n    93\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{itemize}\\\\\\\\n    94\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe win predicate $D^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} = D^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ is the function of the tuple $(t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}, x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}, t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}, x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}, a_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}, a_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B})$ determined by the following clauses, evaluated for $w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$; in all cases where no action is indicated, the predicate accepts.\\\\\\\\n    95\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n    96\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\textbf{Consistency test}) If $t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} = t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}$, accept if and only if $a_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} = a_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}$.\\\\\\\\n    97\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source phrases both clauses with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" t such that x = u_0 + tv; that phrase\\\\\\\\n    98\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% does not determine t on diagonal questions with direction v' = 0, which the\\\\\\\\n    99\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% question distribution samples with positive probability. The universal\\\\\\\\n   100\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% quantification over t used here is the local correction; see the remark below.\\\\\\\\n   101\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\textbf{Axis-parallel line-versus-point test}) If $t_w = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ALine}$ and $t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}$, accept if and only if $f_j(t) = (a_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w})_j$ for all $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,k\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and all $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ such that $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t e_i$, where $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$.\\\\\\\\n   102\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\textbf{Diagonal line-versus-point test}) If $t_w = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}$ and $t_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point}$, accept if and only if $f_j(t) = (a_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w})_j$ for all $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,k\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and all $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ such that $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$, where $v' = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ and $i = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\chi(s)$.\\\\\\\\n   103\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   104\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ is the typed game with type set $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal T^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$, the question distribution of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}, and win predicate $D^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}}$.\\\\\\\\n   105\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   106\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   107\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Diagonal questions with zero direction]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}\\\\\\\\n   108\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe source states the two line-versus-point clauses with ``the'' $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ such that $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t v$. When the direction is nonzero and $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w}$ lies on the line, exactly one such $t$ exists and the formulation above agrees with the source. But zero directions occur: Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:line} explicitly permits them, and the question distribution of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} produces $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{DLine}$ questions with $v' = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$ with positive probability, namely whenever the last $m - i + 1$ coordinates of $v$ vanish. In that case $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0, 0) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{u_0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, every $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ satisfies $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$ when $x_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\overline w} = u_0$, and the source clause does not determine acceptance for a nonconstant answer polynomial. The universal quantification over $t$ adopted in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} makes the predicate a well-defined function of its arguments; it accepts vacuously when no such $t$ exists, a case the question distribution never produces. Completeness is unaffected: the restriction of a polynomial $g$ to the singleton line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ell(u_0,0)$, expressed through the parameterization $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mapsto u_0 + t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot 0$, is the constant polynomial with value $g(u_0)$, which passes the check for every $t$. Soundness is likewise unaffected: zero directions occur with probability at most $2/(mq)$, and the proof of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} shows that any acceptance convention on this event changes success probabilities by at most that amount, which is absorbed into the universal constants of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$.\\\\\\\\n   109\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\\\\\n   110\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   111\\\\\\\\tThe soundness properties of the low individual degree game are expressed in terms of the following class of measurements.\\\\\\\\n   112\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   113\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Low-degree polynomial measurements]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:ld-meas}\\\\\\\\n   114\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:polyfunc, def:polymeasurements, def:polynomials-degree}\\\\\\\\n   115\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ denote the set of POVM measurements indexed by $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$, the polynomial representatives of individual degree at most $d$ in each variable; this is the notion of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:polymeasurements}, and the argument order $(m,q,d)$ follows that definition (the paper orders the arguments as $(m,d,q)$). By Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:polynomials-degree}, evaluation identifies these representatives with the corresponding polynomial functions $g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, bijectively whenever $d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le q-1$; the parameter regimes of this chapter (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:introparams} fixes $d = 1$, and admissibility gives $q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 2$) always satisfy this. More generally, for an integer $k$ and tuples $m = (m_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m_k)$, $q = (q_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,q_k)$, $d = (d_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,d_k)$, let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$ denote the set of measurements $G = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{G_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ such that for each $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,k\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, the outcome index $g_i$ is a polynomial $g_i\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_{q_i}^{m_i} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_{q_i}$ of individual degree at most $d_i$.\\\\\\\\n   116\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   117\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   118\\\\\\\\tQuantum soundness of a version of the classical low-degree test for polynomials of low \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{total} degree was claimed by Natarajan and Vidick, building on an analysis of a three-prover version of the test by Vidick; there is a known gap in the soundness analysis of that test, and it is not used here. Instead, the construction relies on the low \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{individual} degree test, whose quantum soundness is proved in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} and generalized in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}. The statement below is the form most directly useful in what follows.\\\\\\\\n   119\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   120\\\\\\\\t% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.\\\\\\\\n   121\\\\\\\\t% We keep both the environment and the label verbatim for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\\\\\n   122\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{theorem}[Quantum soundness of the simultaneous classical low individual degree test]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:ld-soundness}\\\\\\\\n   123\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:projective-strategy-general, def:tensor-product-value, def:povm-distance, def:bracket}\\\\\\\\n   124\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThere exists a function\\\\\\\\n   125\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   126\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps, q, m, d, k) = a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,(dmk)^a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\n   127\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   128\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tfor universal constants $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ and $0 < b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 1$ such that the following holds. For all $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps > 0$ and every parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ as in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-game} (so $m, d, k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$, $q$ is an admissible field size, and $m$ divides $q$), and for all projective strategies $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi, A, B)$ that succeed with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$ in the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}}$, there exist measurements\\\\\\\\n   129\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   130\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tG^w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)\\\\\\\\n   131\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   132\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ton $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hilb_w$ for $w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, where by slight abuse of notation $m$, $q$, $d$ denote the constant tuples $(m,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m)$, $(q,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,q)$, $(d,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,d)$ of length $k$, such that\\\\\\\\n   133\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   134\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u}_{a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}\\\\\\\\n   135\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}}\\\\\\\\n   136\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tI_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}_{[(g_1(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k)]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   137\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tG^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}_{[(g_1(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k)]} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}\\\\\\\\n   138\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}}\\\\\\\\n   139\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tI_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Point},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u}_{a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_k}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   140\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tG^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A}_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}\\\\\\\\n   141\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}}\\\\\\\\n   142\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tI_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_k}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n   143\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   144\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps,q,m,d,k)$, all three approximations are with respect to the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$, and the bracket notation for post-processed outcomes is that of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:bracket}. The first two approximations hold on average over the uniform distribution on $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$; no question distribution is associated with the third.\\\\\\\\n   145\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{theorem}\\\\\\\\n   146\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   147\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   148\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma^q$ be the Reed--Solomon code of degree $d$: the set of all $(p(x_1),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,p(x_q))$ where $p\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ is a univariate polynomial of degree at most $d$ and $x_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,x_q$ enumerate $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$. We may assume $d < q$. In the opposite regime the statement is immediate: for $d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge q$ the prefactor gives $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,(dmk)^a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, q^{-b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge q^{a-b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$, using $m, k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$, while the quantity bounded in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-distance} never exceeds $2$ for families of POVM elements, so the three approximations hold for an arbitrary choice of $G^w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$. For $d < q$, evaluation is injective on polynomials of degree at most $d$ and a nonzero polynomial of degree at most $d$ vanishes at no more than $d$ of the $q$ points, so $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code$ is a linear $[q,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, d+1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, q-d]_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma$ code.\\\\\\\\n   149\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the\\\\\\\\n   150\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% intended value n = q the printed distance q-d+1 exceeds the true minimum distance\\\\\\\\n   151\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% q-d of the degree-d evaluation code, and the dimension claim d+1 requires d < q.\\\\\\\\n   152\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% Both points are corrected above; the regime d >= q, which the parameter tuple of\\\\\\\\n   153\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% Definition def:ld-game permits, is handled by the triviality argument. The\\\\\\\\n   154\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% boundary values d = 0 and k = 0 are excluded by the parameter domain of\\\\\\\\n   155\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% Definition def:ld-game, following the source's convention that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N denotes\\\\\\\\n   156\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% the positive integers.\\\\\\\\n   157\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source asserts a flat identity between the two tests\\\\\\\\n   158\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:446-454).\\\\\\\\n   159\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% After the zero-direction correction of Definition def:ld-win-predicate\\\\\\\\n   160\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (see rem:ld-win-zero-direction) the identity holds only away from\\\\\\\\n   161\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% zero-direction diagonal questions, so the import is scoped to the predicate\\\\\\\\n   162\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% analyzed in the external source and bridged by the probability estimate below,\\\\\\\\n   163\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% which is insensitive to the external test's acceptance convention on that event.\\\\\\\\n   164\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, agrees with the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$ on every question pair whose diagonal-line direction is nonzero: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. On diagonal line-versus-point questions with direction $v' = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$, the win predicate of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} resolves an ambiguity of the source (Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}), and the resolution is not required to match the acceptance convention of the subcube commutation test on this event: the event has probability at most $2/(mq)$ under the question distribution, since the direction $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ vanishes with probability $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac1m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{j=1}^m q^{-j} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{m(q-1)} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{2}{mq}$ over uniform $(s,v)$, so the success probabilities of any fixed strategy in the two games differ by at most $2/(mq)$. A projective strategy that succeeds in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$ therefore succeeds in the tensor-code test with probability at least $1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps - 2/(mq)$. Theorem~4.7 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{ldparams}$) and applied with the success deficit $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps + 2/(mq)$ in place of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, then yields the present statement for $k = 1$: since $0 < b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 1$ and $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ give $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps + 2/(mq))^b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b + 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,q^{-b}$, the additional error is absorbed into $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}$ by enlarging the universal constant $a$, using $dmk \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.\\\\\\\\n   165\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   166\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   167\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Relation to the low individual degree test of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:test}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:ld-soundness-provider}\\\\\\\\n   168\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tTheorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is imported: the proof above reduces it to Theorem~4.7 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$, followed by the extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The result of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} that underlies it is proved here as Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}: for every projective strategy passing the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, that theorem produces measurements in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ satisfying consistency relations of the same shape as the three approximations of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is not a consequence of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal} alone: a derivation from it would require, in addition, an equivalence between the typed game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ld}}_{(q,m,d,1)}$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:test} --- matching the two question distributions, the two win conditions, and the two error functions --- together with the extension from $k = 1$ to general $k$ by the reduction of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} cited above. Neither implication is proved in this chapter, and the proof of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} given above does not invoke Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}.\\\\\\\\n   169\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\\\\\n   170\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   171\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\section{The Magic Square game}\\\\\\\\n   172\\\\\\\\t% Paper label sec:ms kept for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\\\\\n   173\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:ms}\\\\\\\\n   174\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   175\\\\\\\\tWe recall the Magic Square game of Mermin and Peres. It is a simple self-test for two EPR pairs, and it allows one to test that a pair of binary observables \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{anticommutes}; both properties are used in the Pauli basis test. Here it is presented as a binary constraint satisfaction game. Throughout this section and the next, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ denote the single-qubit Pauli observables, with associated two-outcome projective measurements $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^W_b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2}$ for $W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\\\\\n   176\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   177\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[The Magic Square game]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:ms-game}\\\\\\\\n   178\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:game, def:graph-distribution}\\\\\\\\n   179\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe Magic Square game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{MS}}$ is the following two-player game. There are $9$ variables $x_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,x_9$ taking values in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2$, arranged in a $3 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times 3$ grid: the cell in row $r$ and column $c$ contains the variable $x_{3(r-1)+c}$. There are $6$ linear equations over these variables: for each of the three rows and each of the first two columns, the sum of the three variables in it equals $0$; for the third column, the sum of the three variables in it equals $1$.\\\\\\\\n   180\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   430\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\section{Canonical parameters of the Pauli basis test}\\\\\\\\n   431\\\\\\\\t% Paper label sec:qld-complexity kept for cross-reference fidelity (the complexity\\\\\\\\n   432\\\\\\\\t% statements of that subsection are outside the scope of this development).\\\\\\\\n   433\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:qld-complexity}\\\\\\\\n   434\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   435\\\\\\\\tWe specify a canonical setting of the parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{qldparams}$ as a function of an integer $R$, and bound the resulting soundness function. All logarithms are base $2$.\\\\\\\\n   436\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   437\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Canonical parameters of the Pauli basis test]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:introparams}\\\\\\\\n   438\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{thm:pauli}\\\\\\\\n   439\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $a, b$ be the universal constants of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:pauli}, and let $c$ be the smallest \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{even} integer that is at least $(b+a)/b$. For every integer $R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 4$, define the tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{introparams}(R) = (q, m, d)$ by\\\\\\\\n   440\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{itemize}\\\\\\\\n   441\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item $q = 2^k$ for $k = c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lceil \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rceil + 1$,\\\\\\\\n   442\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item $m = 2^j$, where $j$ is the unique integer with $2^j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lceil \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rceil + 1 < 2^{j+1}$,\\\\\\\\n   443\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item $d = 1$.\\\\\\\\n   444\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{itemize}\\\\\\\\n   445\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe letter $k$ here denotes the exponent of the field size and is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of the classical game. With this choice the Pauli basis test certifies the presence of an $M = 2^m$-qudit maximally entangled state of local dimension $q$, equivalently an $(M \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q)$-qubit EPR state, and the choice $d = 1$ runs the low individual degree test multilinearly.\\\\\\\\n   446\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   447\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   448\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Soundness bound for the canonical parameters]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:delta-bound}\\\\\\\\n   449\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:introparams, def:admissible, thm:pauli}\\\\\\\\n   450\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFor all integers $R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 4$, the parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{introparams}(R) = (q,m,d)$ is admissible (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:admissible}) and satisfies $2^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge R$. Furthermore, there exist universal constants $a' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$ and $0 < b' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 1$ such that the function $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps, m, d, q)$ of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:pauli} is at most\\\\\\\\n   451\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   452\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ta' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl( (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^{a'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^{b'} + (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^{-b'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;.\\\\\\\\n   453\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   454\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   455\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   456\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:introparams, def:admissible, def:admissible-size, thm:pauli}\\\\\\\\n   457\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tSince $c$ is even, the exponent $k = c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lceil \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rceil + 1$ is odd, so $q = 2^k$ is an admissible field size; $m$ divides $q$ because $2^j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lceil \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rceil + 1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log^c R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 2^k$, so $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le k$ and both are powers of $2$; and $d = 1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 1$. Hence the tuple is admissible. Since $m = 2^j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge (c/2) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R$, we get $2^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge R^{c/2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge R$, using $c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 2$.\\\\\\\\n   458\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   459\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFor the soundness bound, write $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps,m,d,q) = a(md)^a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b + a(md)^a q^{-b} + a(md)^a 2^{-bmd}$ and use $d = 1$, $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 2c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R$, and $q = 2^k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log^c R$. The first term is at most $a(2c)^a (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b$. The second term is at most $a(2c)^a (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^{a - cb} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le a(2c)^a (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^{-b}$, since $cb - a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge b$ by the choice of $c$. For the third term, $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge (c/2) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R$ gives $2^{-bm} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le R^{-b}$, so\\\\\\\\n   460\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   461\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ta(md)^a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, 2^{-bm}\\\\\\\\n   462\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le a(2c)^a (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^a R^{-b}\\\\\\\\n   463\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t= a(2c)^a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl( (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^{a+b} R^{-b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr) (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^{-b}\\\\\\\\n   464\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le a(2c)^a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, C\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R)^{-b}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n   465\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   466\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere $C = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sup_{R' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge 4} (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R')^{a+b} (R')^{-b}$ is finite --- the function is continuous in $R'$ and tends to $0$ as $R' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\infty$ --- and depends only on the universal constants $a$ and $b$. Summing the three bounds, the statement follows with $a' = a(2c)^a (1 + C)$ and $b' = b$.\\\\\\\\n   467\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   468\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   469\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[A comparison of exponents in the source proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:delta-bound}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:delta-bound-exponent-comparison}\\\\\\\\n   470\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe source proof bounds the third term of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}}$ by asserting $2^{-bmd} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le q^{-b}$ ``because $k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le m$'', comparing the field exponent $k = c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lceil \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rceil + 1$ with $m = 2^j$. The inequality $k \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le m$ does not follow from Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:introparams}: if the universal constants of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:pauli} were $a = 1$ and $b = 1/2$, then $c = 4$, and $R = 5$ gives $k = 9$ and $m = 8$. The proof above instead bounds $2^{-bm} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le R^{-b}$ directly from $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge (c/2) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ge \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log R$, which yields the same conclusion after enlarging the universal constant $a'$.\\\\\\\\n   471\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\\\\\n     1\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\section{Classical and Quantum Low-degree Tests}\\\\\\\\n     2\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:ldt}\\\\\\\\n     3\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n     4\\\\\\\\tIn this section we introduce the classical and quantum low individual degree tests, that form the basis of our classical, quantum-sound and quantum PCP constructions. For quantum soundness of the classical test we refer to~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{ML20}. For the quantum test, we refer to~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{natarajan2018low} and its adaptation to the low individual degree test given in Appendix~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:qld-analysis}. \\\\\\\\n     5\\\\\\\\tThe protocol in this work combines both of these tests, using the quantum\\\\\\\\n     6\\\\\\\\tlow individual degree test for question reduction and the classical low individual degree test for\\\\\\\\n     7\\\\\\\\tanswer reduction.\\\\\\\\n     8\\\\\\\\tSection~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ld-verifier} below introduces the classical test,\\\\\\\\n     9\\\\\\\\tand Section~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:pauli-verifier} does the same for the quantum \\\\\\\\n    10\\\\\\\\ttest.\\\\\\\\n    11\\\\\\\\tPrior to doing this, we introduce the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{Magic Square game} in\\\\\\\\n    12\\\\\\\\tSection~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ms}, a key subroutine in the quantum test.\\\\\\\\n    13\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    14\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    15\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subsection{The classical low-degree test}\\\\\\\\n    16\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:ld-verifier}\\\\\\\\n    17\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    18\\\\\\\\tWe begin with a generalization of the classical low-degree test known as the\\\\\\\\n    19\\\\\\\\t``simultaneous individual low-degree test''.\\\\\\\\n    20\\\\\\\\tWe sometimes refer to this as the ``classical low-degree test'' for short.\\\\\\\\n    21\\\\\\\\tThe low-degree test is used as a subroutine in the Pauli Basis test (see\\\\\\\\n    22\\\\\\\\tSection~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:pauli-verifier}) as well as the answer-reduction normal form\\\\\\\\n    23\\\\\\\\tverifier (see Section~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ans}).\\\\\\\\n    24\\\\\\\\tWe describe the test as a nonlocal game in Section~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ld-game} and show\\\\\\\\n    25\\\\\\\\tthat the question distribution can be implemented as a (typed) conditionally\\\\\\\\n    26\\\\\\\\tlinear distribution (see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{sec:types} for the definition of typed CL\\\\\\\\n    27\\\\\\\\tdistributions).\\\\\\\\n    28\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    29\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    30\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subsubsection{The game}\\\\\\\\n    31\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:ld-game}\\\\\\\\n    32\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    33\\\\\\\\tThe game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld$ is parametrized by a tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams = (q, m, d, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc)$\\\\\\\\n    34\\\\\\\\twhere $m, d, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N$ are integers, $q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N$ is an admissible field size,\\\\\\\\n    35\\\\\\\\tand $m$ divides $q$.\\\\\\\\n    36\\\\\\\\tWe sometimes write $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams$ to emphasize the dependence of the\\\\\\\\n    37\\\\\\\\tclassical low-degree test on the parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams$.\\\\\\\\n    38\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    39\\\\\\\\tWe first provide a high-level description of the game for $k=1$.\\\\\\\\n    40\\\\\\\\tIt is based on the low-individual degree variant of the multilinearity test\\\\\\\\n    41\\\\\\\\tof~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{babai1991non}, where one player (called the ``points player'') receives\\\\\\\\n    42\\\\\\\\ta random point $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$, and the other player (called the ``lines\\\\\\\\n    43\\\\\\\\tplayer'') receives a random axis-parallel line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line$ that contains $u$, i.e.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    44\\\\\\\\tthe line parallel to the $i$-th axis which goes through point $u$, for uniformly\\\\\\\\n    45\\\\\\\\trandom $i\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\\\\\n    46\\\\\\\\tThe points player is supposed to respond with a value $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, and the\\\\\\\\n    47\\\\\\\\tlines player is supposed to respond with a univariate polynomial $f_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line$ of\\\\\\\\n    48\\\\\\\\tdegree at most $d$ such that $f_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line(u_i) = a$, where $u_i$ is the $i$-th\\\\\\\\n    49\\\\\\\\tcoordinate of $u$.\\\\\\\\n    50\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    51\\\\\\\\tSuppose the players agree beforehand on a global polynomial $g: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$\\\\\\\\n    52\\\\\\\\tsuch that every variable has degree at most $d$.\\\\\\\\n    53\\\\\\\\tThen a winning strategy is the following: the points player responds with\\\\\\\\n    54\\\\\\\\t$g(u)$, and the lines player responds with the univariate polynomial $f_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line$\\\\\\\\n    55\\\\\\\\tthat is the restriction of $g$ to line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line$; formally, for $t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$,\\\\\\\\n    56\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[f_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line(t)=g(u_1,u_2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,u_{i-1},t,u_{i+1},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,u_m)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\] It is easy to\\\\\\\\n    57\\\\\\\\tsee that $f_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line(t)$ has degree at most $d$ in the variable $t$.\\\\\\\\n    58\\\\\\\\tThus in this case the players will pass the low-degree test with probability\\\\\\\\n    59\\\\\\\\t$1$.\\\\\\\\n    60\\\\\\\\tThe low-degree testing theorem states that the converse approximately holds: if\\\\\\\\n    61\\\\\\\\tthe players pass the low-degree test with probability close to $1$, then their\\\\\\\\n    62\\\\\\\\tresponses must be approximately consistent (in some sense) with a global\\\\\\\\n    63\\\\\\\\tpolynomial $g$ with individual degree $d$.\\\\\\\\n    64\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    65\\\\\\\\tWhen $k > 1$, the players are supposed to respond with a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{tuple} of\\\\\\\\n    66\\\\\\\\tanswers; and the test is intended to check that the players' responses are\\\\\\\\n    67\\\\\\\\tconsistent with a $k$-tuple of functions $g_i: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ that are\\\\\\\\n    68\\\\\\\\tpolynomials where every variable has degree at most $d$.\\\\\\\\n    69\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    70\\\\\\\\tThe game that we use is slightly more elaborate than the ``axis-parallel\\\\\\\\n    71\\\\\\\\tlines-versus-points'' test just described.\\\\\\\\n    72\\\\\\\\tIn addition, we have two other subtests.\\\\\\\\n    73\\\\\\\\tOne is a ``diagonal line-vs-points'' test: here, the points player receives a\\\\\\\\n    74\\\\\\\\tpoint $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ as before, but the lines player receives a line $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\line$\\\\\\\\n    75\\\\\\\\tthat goes through $u$ but is not necessarily axis-aligned.\\\\\\\\n    76\\\\\\\\tInstead, it's chosen by first picking an index $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$\\\\\\\\n    77\\\\\\\\tuniformly at random, picking $v \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ uniformly at random, and letting $v'\\\\\\\\n    78\\\\\\\\t= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v) = (0,0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,0,v_i,v_{i+1},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,v_m)$ (i.e., $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}$\\\\\\\\n    79\\\\\\\\tzeroes out the first $i-1$ coordinates of its input).\\\\\\\\n    80\\\\\\\\tThen, the line is defined as the set\\\\\\\\n   420\\\\\\\\t  $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$ in the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams$, there exists measurements\\\\\\\\n   421\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[G^w \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simulpolymeas{m}{d}{q}{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\] on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_w$, for\\\\\\\\n   422\\\\\\\\t  $w\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, such that\\\\\\\\n   423\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   424\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{split}\\\\\\\\n   425\\\\\\\\t      A^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Point,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u}_{a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc}\\\\\\\\n   426\\\\\\\\t      & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\abc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld}]\\\\\\\\n   427\\\\\\\\t      G^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob_{[(g_1(u),g_2(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc(u)) = (a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc)]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   428\\\\\\\\t      G^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice_{[(g_1(u),g_2(u),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,g_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc(u)) = (a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc)]}\\\\\\\\n   429\\\\\\\\t      & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\abc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld}]\\\\\\\\n   430\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t B^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Point,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u}_{a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, a_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   431\\\\\\\\t      G^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, g_2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, g_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc}\\\\\\\\n   432\\\\\\\\t      & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\abc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld}]\\\\\\\\n   433\\\\\\\\t      G^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob_{g_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, g_2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, g_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n   434\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{split}\\\\\\\\n   435\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   436\\\\\\\\t  where $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps, q, m, d, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc)$, \\\\\\\\n   437\\\\\\\\t  and all three statements are with respect to the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$.\\\\\\\\n   438\\\\\\\\tIn addition, the first two approximations hold under the uniform distribution over $u\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$,\\\\\\\\n   439\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhereas there is no question distribution associated with the third approximation.\\\\\\\\n   440\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{theorem}\\\\\\\\n   441\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   442\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   443\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams = (q, m, d, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc)$\\\\\\\\n   444\\\\\\\\twhere $m, d, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N$ are integers, $q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N$ is an admissible field size,\\\\\\\\n   445\\\\\\\\tand $m$ divides $q$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma^q$ be the Reed-Solomon code with degree $d$. Explicitly, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code$ is the set of all $(p(x_1),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,p(x_n))$ where $p:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ is a univariate polynomial of degree at most $d$ and $x_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,x_n$ are an enumeration of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$. Then in the (standard) notation from~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{ji2021quantum} $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code$ is a linear $[q,d+1,n-d+1]_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Sigma$ code.\\\\\\\\n   446\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   447\\\\\\\\tObserve that the two-prover tensor code test, for the code $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\code^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes m}$, as described in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite[Figure 1 and Figure 2]{ji2021quantum}, is identical to the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld$ with parameters $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc=1$, whose decider is presented in Figure~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fig:ld-decider}. This is because \\\\\\\\n   448\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   449\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{axis-parallel line-versus-point test} from Figure~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fig:ld-decider} is identical to the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{axis-parallel lines test} from~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite[Figure 1]{ji2021quantum}, with the only difference that in Figure~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fig:ld-decider} the roles of A and B are chosen uniformly at random. This is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor code test from~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite[Section 4.1]{ji2021quantum}.\\\\\\\\n   450\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{diagonal line-versus-point test} from Figure~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fig:ld-decider} is identical to the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{subcube commutation test} from~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite[Figure 1]{ji2021quantum}, with again the added symmetrization between players. \\\\\\\\n   451\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{consistency test} from Figure~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fig:ld-decider} is identical to the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{synchronicity test} from~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite[Figure 2]{ji2021quantum}.\\\\\\\\n   452\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   453\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   454\\\\\\\\tAs a result,~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite[Theorem 4.7]{ji2021quantum}, in which we choose the parameter $k$ as $k=m^3d$,  immediately yields \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{lem:ld-soundness}.\\\\\\\\n   455\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   456\\\\\\\\tTo conclude it remains to extend the result to the case of general $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldc$. This is done \\\\\\\\n   457\\\\\\\\tvia a standard reduction, following exactly the same steps as the derivation of Theorem 4.43 from Theorem 4.40 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{NW19}.\\\\\\\\n   458\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   459\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   460\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   461\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   462\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   463\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   464\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   465\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subsubsection{Complexity of the classical low-degree test.}\\\\\\\\n   466\\\\\\\\tThe CL functions and decision procedure of the low-degree test are incorporated\\\\\\\\n   467\\\\\\\\tas subroutines in some of the normal form verifiers constructed in subsequent\\\\\\\\n   468\\\\\\\\tsections.\\\\\\\\n   469\\\\\\\\tThe next lemma establishes the time complexity of these procedures as a function\\\\\\\\n   470\\\\\\\\tof the parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams = (q,m,d,k)$.\\\\\\\\n   471\\\\\\\\tThe lemma also establishes the time complexity of computing the {description} of\\\\\\\\n   472\\\\\\\\tthe decision procedure $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\decider^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ld$ as a Turing machine, given the parameter\\\\\\\\n   473\\\\\\\\ttuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldparams$ as input.\\\\\\\\n   474\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   475\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1450\\\\\\\\tThe strategies described in the proof of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{lem:pauli-completeness} and in the conclusion of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{thm:pauli} are described in terms of qudit Pauli measurements and maximally entangled states defined over $q$-dimensional qudits. \\\\\\\\n  1451\\\\\\\\tHowever, it is more convenient for our application of the Pauli basis test to\\\\\\\\n  1452\\\\\\\\tinstead deal with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{qubits}.\\\\\\\\n  1453\\\\\\\\tIn particular, we use the Pauli basis test in the ``introspection game'' of\\\\\\\\n  1454\\\\\\\\tSection~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:introspection}, where the players are commanded to sample\\\\\\\\n  1455\\\\\\\\tquestions according to a sampler $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sampler$ of a normal form verifier.\\\\\\\\n  1456\\\\\\\\tBy definition of normal form verifier, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sampler$ is a sampler over $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2$, and\\\\\\\\n  1457\\\\\\\\ttherefore it is natural to use a test for qubit Pauli measurements.\\\\\\\\n  1458\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1459\\\\\\\\tSince we use field sizes $q$ that are powers of $2$, Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:pauli-binary}\\\\\\\\n  1460\\\\\\\\tshows that the conclusions of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{thm:pauli} can also be described in terms of\\\\\\\\n  1461\\\\\\\\ttesting for maximally entangled states over qubits and qubit Pauli measurements.\\\\\\\\n  1462\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1463\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1464\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1465\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1466\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1467\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1468\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1469\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{corollary}\\\\\\\\n  1470\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{cor:pauli-binary}  \\\\\\\\n  1471\\\\\\\\tThere exists a function\\\\\\\\n  1472\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qld}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps,q,m,d) = a(md)^a(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n  1473\\\\\\\\tfor universal constants $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1$ and $0 < b < 1$ such that the following holds. For all admissible parameter tuples $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qldparams = (q,m,d)$ and for all strategies $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi},A,B)$ for the game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pauli_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qldparams$ that succeeds with probability at least $1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, there exist local isometries $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice''},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob''}$ (where $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice''},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cong (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\C^2)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q}$ with $M = 2^m$) and a state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\aux} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob'}$ such that \\\\\\\\n  1474\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n  1475\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\left \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\aux} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q}  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\right \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qld}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps,m,d,q)$,\\\\\\\\n  1476\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item Letting $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{A}^x_a = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_A^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{B}^y_b = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_B B^y_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_B^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger$, we have for $W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$\\\\\\\\n  1477\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{gather*}\\\\\\\\n  1478\\\\\\\\t        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{A}^{(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pauli,W)}_u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qld}} (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^W_u)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  1479\\\\\\\\t        I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{B}^{(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pauli,W)}_u    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qld}} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice'' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^W_u)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob''}\\\\\\\\n  1480\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{gather*}\\\\\\\\n  1481\\\\\\\\t    where the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qld}}$ statement holds with respect to the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\aux}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q}_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice''\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob''}$ and the answer summation is over $u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^M$. Here, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^W_u$ denotes the tensor product of single-qubit Pauli measurements\\\\\\\\n  1482\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n  1483\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigotimes_{i=1}^{M} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigotimes_{j=1}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^W_{u_{ij}}\\\\\\\\n  1484\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n  1485\\\\\\\\t    where $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\kappa(u_i) = (u_{ij})_{1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q}$ (using the bijection $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\kappa: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q}$ from \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{sec:finite-fields}).\\\\\\\\n  1486\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}  \\\\\\\\n  1487\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{corollary}\\\\\\\\n  1488\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1489\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n  1490\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{lem:pauli-binary} implies that there are local isometries that map $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M}$ to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q}$, and map the generalized Pauli measurements $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^W_u$ to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigotimes_{i=1}^M \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigotimes_{j=1}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^W_{u_{ij}}$ where $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\kappa(u_i) = (u_{i1},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,u_{i\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\log q})$. Combining this with the isometries guaranteed by \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{thm:pauli}, we obtain the statement of the Corollary.\\\\\\\\n  1491\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n  1492\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subsubsection{Canonical parameters and complexity of the Pauli basis test}\\\\\\\\n  1493\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:qld-complexity}\\\\\\\\n  1494\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n  1495\\\\\\\\tWe specify a canonical setting of the parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\qldparams$ as a function\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-01.jsonl:238:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_122\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex | sed -n '215,258p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex | sed -n '650,850p'; nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '128,218p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '62,100p'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"   215\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere, for operators $M$, $N$ that are not POVM elements, the notation $M \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta N$ means $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (M-N)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger (M-N) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)$ with respect to the same state.\\\\\\\\n   216\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{theorem}\\\\\\\\n   217\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   218\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThis result is imported from Theorem~6.9 of~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Coladangelo2017Robust}. There are two minor differences between their statement and the one given here. First, they state a robustness of $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps)$, but measure the closeness of the actual and ideal states in trace norm; expressed as the Euclidean distance between $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm B}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot 2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{aux}}$, this becomes $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps)$. Second, their ideal observables for the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Variable}_1$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Variable}_5$ questions are $I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$; under a local change of basis these are equivalent to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I$, respectively.\\\\\\\\n   219\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   220\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   221\\\\\\\\tThe converse construction shows that any pair of anticommuting binary observables yields a perfect strategy for the Magic Square game.\\\\\\\\n   222\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   223\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{theorem}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{thm:ms-from-ac}\\\\\\\\n   224\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:ms-game, def:consistent-measurement, def:spcc, def:EPR, def:tensor-product-value}\\\\\\\\n   225\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $A = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A_b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2}$ and $B = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B_b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2}$ be two-outcome projective measurements acting on $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\C^q)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot n}$ that are consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot n}$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:consistent-measurement}), and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A = A_0 - A_1$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B = B_0 - B_1$ be the corresponding $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observables. Suppose that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B = - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A$. Then there exists a symmetric strategy $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal S = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi, M)$ for the Magic Square game with the following properties:\\\\\\\\n   226\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   227\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal S$ is an SPCC (symmetric, projective, consistent, commuting) strategy of value $1$;\\\\\\\\n   228\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item the state is $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$;\\\\\\\\n   229\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item for $b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, $M^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Variable}_1}_b = A_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I$ and $M^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Variable}_5}_b = B_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I$.\\\\\\\\n   230\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   231\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{theorem}\\\\\\\\n   232\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   233\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:consistent-measurement}\\\\\\\\n   234\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe strategy is based on the canonical two-qubit operator solution of the Magic Square, with the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$ and the cells of the grid assigned the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observables\\\\\\\\n   235\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   236\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{array}{ccc}\\\\\\\\n   237\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I & I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[2pt]\\\\\\\\n   238\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tI \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[2pt]\\\\\\\\n   239\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X\\\\\\\\n   240\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\n   241\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   242\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ton $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\C^q)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\C^2$: on question $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Variable}_j$ a player measures the two-outcome projective measurement of the observable in cell $j$, and on question $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Constraint}_i$ a player simultaneously performs the three measurements of the corresponding row or column; item~3 holds by construction. Anticommutation of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B$ gives\\\\\\\\n   243\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:am-i-hermitian}\\\\\\\\n   244\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X\\\\\\\\n   245\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t= - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z\\\\\\\\n   246\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z\\\\\\\\n   247\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t= (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n   248\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n   249\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tso the bottom-right cell is Hermitian, and it squares to the identity; hence every cell is a genuine $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observable. The three observables in each row and each column commute pairwise --- for the third row and third column this again uses Equation~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:am-i-hermitian} --- so the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Constraint}$ measurements are well defined, and the strategy is symmetric, projective, and commuting. A two-outcome projective measurement is consistent on a state if and only if its observable $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O$ satisfies $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal O \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$; since $A$ and $B$ are consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot n}$ by hypothesis and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ are consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$, each cell observable is a product of consistent, commuting factors and is therefore consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$, and consistency of the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Constraint}$ measurements follows from the cellwise consistencies together with commutativity. Finally, the observables in each row and each of the first two columns multiply to $I$, while those of the third column multiply to $-I$; for the measurement $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{S_0, S_1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ obtained by measuring a $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Constraint}_i$ triple and outputting the sum of the three bits, the observable $S_0 - S_1$ equals the product of the three cell observables, hence $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm I$, which forces the three answer bits to sum to $0$ (respectively, $1$ for $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Constraint}_6$) with certainty. Together with consistency this shows that every check of the Magic Square game is passed with probability $1$, so the strategy has value $1$.\\\\\\\\n   250\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   251\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   252\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\section{The Pauli basis test}\\\\\\\\n   253\\\\\\\\t% Paper label sec:qld-game kept for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\\\\\n   254\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{sec:qld-game}\\\\\\\\n   255\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   256\\\\\\\\tThe Pauli basis test is the quantum low individual degree test of Natarajan and Vidick, in the slightly modified form introduced by Natarajan and Wright~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Informally, the test certifies that the players share the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M}$, where $M = 2^m$. On a question of type $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathsf{Pauli}, W)$ with $W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, a player is expected to measure the generalized Pauli basis POVM $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^W_h\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{h \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^M}$ of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra} and report the outcome $h$. Most subtests probe limited information about $h$: given a point $u_W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m$, a player is expected to report the evaluation $g_h(u_W)$ of the low-degree encoding $g_h$ of $h$, and for a fixed basis $W$ these probes are checked against each other by the classical low individual degree game of Section~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ld-verifier}. Consistency \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{between} the two bases is checked through two-outcome probes: given additionally $r_W \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, a player reports $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr(g_h(u_W)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, r_W) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2$. The two binary measurements obtained in this way for $W = X$ and $W = Z$ either commute or anticommute, according to the value $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2$ of Equation~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:gamma-value} below. When $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma = 0$ the verifier checks commutation directly by asking for both bits simultaneously; when $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma = 1$ the verifier checks anticommutation by playing the Magic Square game, using Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac}.\\\\\\\\n   257\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   258\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Admissible parameters]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:admissible}\\\\\\\\n   650\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   651\\\\\\\\t \\\\\\\\tA proof of the rigidity of the Magic Square game can be found in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite[Theorem 6.9]{coladangelo2017robust}. There are a couple minor differences between their rigidity statement and the one stated here. First, they state that the robustness of the Magic Square self-test is $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps)$; however they specify the closeness between the actual and ideal states in terms of the trace norm, whereas we specify the closeness between $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes 2}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\aux}$ in terms of the Euclidean distance, which translates to $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps})$ instead of $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps)$. Second, their choice of ideal strategy specifies that the observables corresponding to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_1$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_5$ questions are $I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^{Z} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^{X} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$; however under a local change of basis these are equivalent to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I$ respectively.\\\\\\\\n   652\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   653\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   654\\\\\\\\tWe will need the following theorem,\\\\\\\\n   655\\\\\\\\twhich shows that any pair of anticommuting observables \\\\\\\\n   656\\\\\\\\tcan be used to form a value-$1$ strategy for the Magic Square game.\\\\\\\\n   657\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   658\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{theorem}\\\\\\\\n   659\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{thm:ms-from-ac}\\\\\\\\n   660\\\\\\\\t  Let $A = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A_b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2}$ and $B = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B_b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2}$ be two-outcome\\\\\\\\n   661\\\\\\\\t  projective measurements acting on $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\C^q)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n}$ which are consistent on\\\\\\\\n   662\\\\\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n}$, and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}_A = A_0 - A_1$ and\\\\\\\\n   663\\\\\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}_B = B_0 - B_1$ be the corresponding observables.\\\\\\\\n   664\\\\\\\\t  Suppose that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}_B = -\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}_A$.\\\\\\\\n   665\\\\\\\\t  Then there exists a symmetric strategy $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi, M)$ for the Magic\\\\\\\\n   666\\\\\\\\t  Square game with the following properties.\\\\\\\\n   667\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   668\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy$ is an SPCC strategy of value $1$.\\\\\\\\n   669\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item The state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ has the form $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes\\\\\\\\n   670\\\\\\\\t      n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}$.\\\\\\\\n   671\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item For $b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, we have $M^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_1}_b = A_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I$ and\\\\\\\\n   672\\\\\\\\t    $M^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_5}_b = B_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I$.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{item:embeds}\\\\\\\\n   673\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   674\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{theorem}\\\\\\\\n   675\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   676\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   677\\\\\\\\t  The strategy~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy$ is based on the canonical two-qubit strategy for the\\\\\\\\n   678\\\\\\\\t  Magic Square game as described in, for example,~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{aravind2002simple}.\\\\\\\\n   679\\\\\\\\t  The state is $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}$.\\\\\\\\n   680\\\\\\\\t  We specify the measurements of~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy$ in\\\\\\\\n   681\\\\\\\\t  Figure~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fig:ms-operator-soln} as an \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{operator solution} for the Magic\\\\\\\\n   682\\\\\\\\t  Square game, meant to be read as follows: each cell contains a two-outcome\\\\\\\\n   683\\\\\\\\t  projective measurement $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{E_0, E_1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ on $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\C^q)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\C^2$\\\\\\\\n   684\\\\\\\\t  written as its \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observable} $E_0 - E_1$.\\\\\\\\n   685\\\\\\\\t  When Player~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice$ or~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob$ receives the question $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_j$ for $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\n   686\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots, 9\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, they measure their share of~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ using the\\\\\\\\n   687\\\\\\\\t  measurement specified by the cell corresponding to~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_j$ and receive a\\\\\\\\n   688\\\\\\\\t  single-bit measurement.\\\\\\\\n   689\\\\\\\\t  When they receive the question $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Constraint_i$ for $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,6\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$,\\\\\\\\n   690\\\\\\\\t  they simultaneously perform the three measurements in the corresponding row or\\\\\\\\n   691\\\\\\\\t  column on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ to obtain three bits.\\\\\\\\n   692\\\\\\\\t  For example, if Player~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice$ receives question~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_1$, they\\\\\\\\n   693\\\\\\\\t  measure~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ using the measurement $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A_0 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I, A_1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$\\\\\\\\n   694\\\\\\\\t  corresponding to the observable $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I$ (where the first\\\\\\\\n   695\\\\\\\\t  operator acts on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n}$ and the second acts on\\\\\\\\n   696\\\\\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}$).\\\\\\\\n   697\\\\\\\\t  Similarly, on question~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_5$, they use the measurement $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B_0 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes\\\\\\\\n   698\\\\\\\\t  I, B_1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\\\\\n   699\\\\\\\\t  This establishes Item~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{item:embeds} of the theorem.\\\\\\\\n   700\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{figure}[ht!]\\\\\\\\n   701\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{center}\\\\\\\\n   702\\\\\\\\t      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\renewcommand{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\arraystretch}{2.4}\\\\\\\\n   703\\\\\\\\t      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{tabularx}{.6\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\textwidth}{| X | X | X |}\\\\\\\\n   704\\\\\\\\t        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hline\\\\\\\\n   705\\\\\\\\t        $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;O}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;I$\\\\\\\\n   706\\\\\\\\t        & $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_BO} I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$\\\\\\\\n   707\\\\\\\\t        & $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   708\\\\\\\\t        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hline\\\\\\\\n   709\\\\\\\\t        $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_AO} I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$\\\\\\\\n   710\\\\\\\\t        & $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;O}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;I$\\\\\\\\n   711\\\\\\\\t        & $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   712\\\\\\\\t        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hline\\\\\\\\n   713\\\\\\\\t        $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;O}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$\\\\\\\\n   714\\\\\\\\t        & $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phantom{I\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;O}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$\\\\\\\\n   715\\\\\\\\t        & $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   716\\\\\\\\t        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hline\\\\\\\\n   717\\\\\\\\t      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{tabularx}\\\\\\\\n   718\\\\\\\\t      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\caption{Observables for Magic Square strategy}\\\\\\\\n   719\\\\\\\\t      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fig:ms-operator-soln}\\\\\\\\n   720\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{center}\\\\\\\\n   721\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{figure}\\\\\\\\n   722\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\n   723\\\\\\\\t  First, we show that this gives a well-defined strategy.\\\\\\\\n   724\\\\\\\\t  The $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_j$ measurements are well-defined because each cell contains a\\\\\\\\n   725\\\\\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observable.\\\\\\\\n   726\\\\\\\\t  This is obvious for all $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 9$; when $j = 9$, the bottom-right cell\\\\\\\\n   727\\\\\\\\t  contains $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$.\\\\\\\\n   728\\\\\\\\t  Because~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A$ and~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B$ anti-commute,\\\\\\\\n   729\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:am-i-hermitian}\\\\\\\\n   730\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X\\\\\\\\n   731\\\\\\\\t  = - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z\\\\\\\\n   732\\\\\\\\t  = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z\\\\\\\\n   733\\\\\\\\t  = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger.\\\\\\\\n   734\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n   735\\\\\\\\t  As a result, this matrix is Hermitian. In addition, \\\\\\\\n   736\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   737\\\\\\\\t  (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X)^2\\\\\\\\n   738\\\\\\\\t  = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z)\\\\\\\\n   739\\\\\\\\t  = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z)\\\\\\\\n   740\\\\\\\\t  = I,\\\\\\\\n   741\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   742\\\\\\\\t  where the first step uses Equation~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:am-i-hermitian} and the final\\\\\\\\n   743\\\\\\\\t  step uses the fact that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ are $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm\\\\\\\\n   744\\\\\\\\t  1$-valued observables and hence square to the identity.\\\\\\\\n   745\\\\\\\\t  As a result, this matrix is Hermitian and squares to the identity.\\\\\\\\n   746\\\\\\\\t  Therefore, it is a $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observable.\\\\\\\\n   747\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\n   748\\\\\\\\t  As for the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Constraint_i$ measurements,\\\\\\\\n   749\\\\\\\\t  we must show that the three measurements in each row and column are simultaneously measurable.\\\\\\\\n   750\\\\\\\\t  This is equivalent to the three $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observables being simultaneously diagonalizable,\\\\\\\\n   751\\\\\\\\t  which is equivalent to them being pairwise commuting. This can be easily verified for the cases of $i = 1, 2, 4, 5$\\\\\\\\n   752\\\\\\\\t  (i.e.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ the first two rows and columns).\\\\\\\\n   753\\\\\\\\t  In the case of $i = 3$, commutativity of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$ \\\\\\\\n   754\\\\\\\\t  follows from Equation~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:am-i-hermitian}.\\\\\\\\n   755\\\\\\\\t  Since these two matrices commute, they also commute with their product \\\\\\\\n   756\\\\\\\\t  $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z)(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$.\\\\\\\\n   757\\\\\\\\t  The case of $i = 6$ is similar.\\\\\\\\n   758\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\n   759\\\\\\\\t  By construction, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy$ is symmetric, and we have already shown that it\\\\\\\\n   760\\\\\\\\t  is projective.\\\\\\\\n   761\\\\\\\\t  It remains to show that it is commuting, consistent, and value~$1$.\\\\\\\\n   762\\\\\\\\t  To show that it is commuting, it suffices to show that the measurement for\\\\\\\\n   763\\\\\\\\t  each cell is simultaneously measurable with all three measurements in its row\\\\\\\\n   764\\\\\\\\t  or column, which was already proved above.\\\\\\\\n   765\\\\\\\\t  Now we show consistency of the measurements.\\\\\\\\n   766\\\\\\\\t  We will show this by instead showing consistency of their observables.\\\\\\\\n   767\\\\\\\\t  By this we mean the following: if $E = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{E_0, E_1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ is a two-outcome projective measurement on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{H}$\\\\\\\\n   768\\\\\\\\t  then its corresponding $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observable $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O} = E_0 - E_1$\\\\\\\\n   769\\\\\\\\t  is consistent on a state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{H} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{H}$ if\\\\\\\\n   770\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   771\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi} = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}.\\\\\\\\n   772\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   773\\\\\\\\t  This is in fact equivalent to the notion of $E$ being consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}$ from Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:consistent-measurement}.\\\\\\\\n   774\\\\\\\\t  To see this, if $E$ is consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}$, then so is $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}$, because\\\\\\\\n   775\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   776\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}\\\\\\\\n   777\\\\\\\\t  = (E_0 - E_1) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}\\\\\\\\n   778\\\\\\\\t  = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (E_0 - E_1) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}\\\\\\\\n   779\\\\\\\\t  = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi},\\\\\\\\n   780\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   781\\\\\\\\t  where the second equality is by consistency of~$E$.\\\\\\\\n   782\\\\\\\\t  On the other hand, suppose $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}$ is consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}$.\\\\\\\\n   783\\\\\\\\t  Then because $E_0 + E_1 = I$, we can write $E_0 = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (I + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O})$ and $E_1 = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (I - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O})$.\\\\\\\\n   784\\\\\\\\t  As a result,\\\\\\\\n   785\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   786\\\\\\\\t  E_0 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}\\\\\\\\n   787\\\\\\\\t  = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (I + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}\\\\\\\\n   788\\\\\\\\t  = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (I + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}\\\\\\\\n   789\\\\\\\\t  = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes E_0 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi},\\\\\\\\n   790\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   791\\\\\\\\t  and similarly for $E_0$. Thus, $E$ is consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi}$.\\\\\\\\n   792\\\\\\\\t  This proves the equivalence.\\\\\\\\n   793\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\n   794\\\\\\\\t  Now we use this to prove consistency of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy$.\\\\\\\\n   795\\\\\\\\t  To begin, we note that because $A$ and $B$ are consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n}$ by assumption,\\\\\\\\n   796\\\\\\\\t  so to are their $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\pm 1$-valued observables $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B$.\\\\\\\\n   797\\\\\\\\t  Next, we note that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$ are consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$;\\\\\\\\n   798\\\\\\\\t  to verify this, we recall that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{0} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{1}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{1} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{0}$, and so\\\\\\\\n   799\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   800\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}\\\\\\\\n   801\\\\\\\\t&  =   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac{1}{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{2}} (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{00} + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{11})\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   802\\\\\\\\t&  = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac{1}{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{2}} (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{10} + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{01})\\\\\\\\n   803\\\\\\\\t    =    I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tfrac{1}{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{2}} (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{11} + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{00})\\\\\\\\n   804\\\\\\\\t    =    I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}.\\\\\\\\n   805\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   806\\\\\\\\t  This shows $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X$ is consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$,\\\\\\\\n   807\\\\\\\\t  and a similar argument shows the same for $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z$.\\\\\\\\n   808\\\\\\\\t  We now use these two facts to show that the observable in each cell of\\\\\\\\n   809\\\\\\\\t  Figure~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fig:ms-operator-soln} is consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$.\\\\\\\\n   810\\\\\\\\t  To see why, consider the $j = 9$ case:\\\\\\\\n   811\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   812\\\\\\\\t&  (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X)_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob\\\\\\\\n   813\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2} 