} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tag{consistency of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A$}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   822\\\\\\\\t  ={}& I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{O}_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sigma^X)_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob\\\\\\\\n   823\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\EPR_2}. \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tag{by Equation~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:am-i-hermitian}}\\\\\\\\n   824\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   825\\\\\\\\t  The remaining cases of $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots, 8\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are similar, and we omit them.\\\\\\\\n   826\\\\\\\\t  This shows that the observable in each cell is consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$,\\\\\\\\n   827\\\\\\\\t  and as a result the corresponding two-outcome projective measurements are consistent as well.\\\\\\\\n   828\\\\\\\\t  As a result, the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable_j$ measurements are consistent.\\\\\\\\n   829\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\n   830\\\\\\\\t  As for the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Constraint_i$ measurements, each such measurement $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{F_{a, b,\\\\\\\\n   831\\\\\\\\t    c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{a, b, c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0, 1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}}$ is of the form $F_{a, b, c} = E^1_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^2_b\\\\\\\\n   832\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^3_c$, where $E^1$, $E^2$, and $E^3$ are $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable$ measurements.\\\\\\\\n   833\\\\\\\\t  But then consistency of~$F$ follows from the~$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable$ consistencies:\\\\\\\\n   834\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   835\\\\\\\\t  F_{a, b, c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\n   836\\\\\\\\t & = (E^1_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^2_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^3_c)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   837\\\\\\\\t & = (E^1_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^2_b)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (E^3_c)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tag{consistency of $E^3$}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   838\\\\\\\\t &   = (E^1_a)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (E^3_c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^2_b)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tag{consistency of $E^2$}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   839\\\\\\\\t &       = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (E^3_c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^2_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^1_a)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tag{consistency of $E^1$}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   840\\\\\\\\t &       = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (E^1_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^2_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot E^2_c)_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tag{commutativity of $E^1$, $E^2$, and $E^3$}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   841\\\\\\\\t &               = I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes F_{a, b,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}.\\\\\\\\n   842\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   843\\\\\\\\t  Hence, all measurements are consistent.\\\\\\\\n   844\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\n   845\\\\\\\\t  Since all measurements are consistent, this implies that the answer bit of the\\\\\\\\n   846\\\\\\\\t  player receiving a $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Variable$ question is always consistent with the\\\\\\\\n   847\\\\\\\\t  corresponding answer bit of the player receiving the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Constraint$ question.\\\\\\\\n   848\\\\\\\\t  Similarly, the answers of the player receiving the $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Constraint$ question\\\\\\\\n   849\\\\\\\\t  always satisfy the given constraint; observe that in all rows and the first\\\\\\\\n   850\\\\\\\\t  two columns, the observables multiply to $I$, whereas the observables in the\\\\\\\\n   128\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tif $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$, and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{commuting} if $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega) = 0$.\\\\\\\\n   129\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   130\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   131\\\\\\\\tThe sign $(-1)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega)}$ is exactly the sign with which the\\\\\\\\n   132\\\\\\\\tgeneralized Pauli observables $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^X(r_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u_X))$ and\\\\\\\\n   133\\\\\\\\t$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tau^Z(r_Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u_Z))$ commute\\\\\\\\n   134\\\\\\\\t(see Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation});\\\\\\\\n   135\\\\\\\\tthis is the source of the terminology.\\\\\\\\n   136\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   137\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Probability of anticommuting tuples]\\\\\\\\n   138\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:omega-anticomm-prob}\\\\\\\\n   139\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source states this result in a `fact' environment; fact environments\\\\\\\\n   140\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% become lemma nodes and the paper label is kept verbatim.\\\\\\\\n   141\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The exact value of the anticommuting probability and the constant lower\\\\\\\\n   142\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% bounds under the hypothesis m <= q are additions relative to the source,\\\\\\\\n   143\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% which asserts only the two bounds displayed at the end of the statement;\\\\\\\\n   144\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% the source states those bounds without the hypothesis m, d >= 1, under\\\\\\\\n   145\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% which alone they hold (see the remark following this lemma). The\\\\\\\\n   146\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% unconditional constant bounds are what the proof of the lemma labelled\\\\\\\\n   147\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% lem:qld-win-implications uses.\\\\\\\\n   148\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:anticommuting-tuple}\\\\\\\\n   149\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega = (u_X,u_Z,r_X,r_Z)$ sampled uniformly at random from\\\\\\\\n   150\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m)^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q)^2$ is anticommuting with probability exactly\\\\\\\\n   151\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   152\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big(1 - q^{-1}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big)^{m+1} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\n   153\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   154\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tand commuting with the complementary probability, which is at least\\\\\\\\n   155\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac12$. In particular, if $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq q$, then $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega$ is anticommuting\\\\\\\\n   156\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twith probability at least $2^{-4}$ and commuting with probability at\\\\\\\\n   157\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tleast $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac12$. If $m, d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1$, then the probability that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega$ is\\\\\\\\n   158\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tanticommuting is at least\\\\\\\\n   159\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   160\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t(1 - q^{-m}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (1 - q^{-1}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{md}{q}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\\\\n   161\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{3md}{q}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\n   162\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   163\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tand the probability that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega$ is commuting is also at least\\\\\\\\n   164\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big(1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{3md}{q}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}$.\\\\\\\\n   165\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   166\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   167\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:low-degree-encoding, def:indicator-vector, def:admissible-size, def:binary-representation, lem:downsize_field, lem:one}\\\\\\\\n   168\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tBy the definition of the indicator vector\\\\\\\\n   169\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t(Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:indicator-vector}), expanding the product over the\\\\\\\\n   170\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tcoordinates $i \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dots, m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ into a sum over $y \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}^m$\\\\\\\\n   171\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tgives\\\\\\\\n   172\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   173\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u_X) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u_Z)\\\\\\\\n   174\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{y \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}^m} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(u_X)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(u_Z)\\\\\\\\n   175\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\prod_{i=1}^{m} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big( u_{X,i}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, u_{Z,i} + (1 - u_{X,i})(1 - u_{Z,i}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\n   176\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   177\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tand since $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ has characteristic $2$, the $i$-th factor equals\\\\\\\\n   178\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$1 + u_{X,i} + u_{Z,i}$. Hence\\\\\\\\n   179\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   180\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big( (r_X r_Z) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\prod_{i=1}^{m}