\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$ is exactly $(1-q^{-1})^{m+1} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac12$, and\\\\\\\\n   199\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tthe probability that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega$ is commuting is the complement, which is\\\\\\\\n   200\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tat least $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac12$.\\\\\\\\n   201\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   202\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tIf $m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq q$, then\\\\\\\\n   203\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$(1-q^{-1})^{m+1} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq (1-q^{-1})^{q+1} = (1-q^{-1})^{q} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (1-q^{-1})$,\\\\\\\\n   204\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ta product of two positive functions of $q$ that are nondecreasing for\\\\\\\\n   205\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 2$, so it is minimized at $q = 2$, where it equals $2^{-3}$;\\\\\\\\n   206\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\thence the anticommuting probability is at least $2^{-4}$.\\\\\\\\n   207\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   208\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFinally, suppose $m, d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1$. By Bernoulli's inequality,\\\\\\\\n   209\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$(1-q^{-1})^{m} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - m/q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - md/q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq (1-q^{-m})(1-md/q)$,\\\\\\\\n   210\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere the last step is trivial when $1 - md/q < 0$; multiplying by\\\\\\\\n   211\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$1 - q^{-1}$ shows that the anticommuting probability is at least the\\\\\\\\n   212\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tfirst displayed bound. For the second inequality of the display, since\\\\\\\\n   213\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$md \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1$ gives $q^{-m} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq q^{-1} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq md/q$, expanding the product\\\\\\\\n   214\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tyields\\\\\\\\n   215\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$(1-q^{-m})(1-q^{-1})(1-md/q) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - q^{-m} - q^{-1} - md/q \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1 - 3md/q$.\\\\\\\\n   216\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe commuting probability is at least\\\\\\\\n   217\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac12 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big(1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{3md}{q}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\big) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac12$.\\\\\\\\n   218\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n    62\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    63\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    64\\\\\\\\tCall a tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega = (u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt,u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt,r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt,r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m)^2\\\\\\\\n    65\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q)^2$ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{anticommuting} if the quantity \\\\\\\\n    66\\\\\\\\t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr ((r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot\\\\\\\\n    67\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt)))\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n    68\\\\\\\\tintroduced in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{eq:gamma-value} is not $0$, and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{commuting} if $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma=0$.\\\\\\\\n    69\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    70\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}\\\\\\\\n    71\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:omega-anticomm-prob}\\\\\\\\n    72\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe probability that a tuple $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega= (u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt,u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt,r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt,r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt)$ sampled uniformly at random from $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^m)^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q)^2$\\\\\\\\n    73\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tis anti-commuting is at least \\\\\\\\n    74\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n    75\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t(1 - q^{-m}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (1 - q^{-1}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big (1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{md}{q} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big )  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big (1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{3md}{q} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big ) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}.\\\\\\\\n    76\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n    77\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe probability that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega$ is commuting is also at least $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(1 - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{3md}{q} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big ) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}$.\\\\\\\\n    78\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n    79\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n    80\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFirst, note that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt) = 0$ if and only if $u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt = 0$. If $u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt=0$ then $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega$ is commuting. Otherwise, if $u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$, then observe that for all $r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt, r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt, u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt$,\\\\\\\\n    81\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n    82\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr( (r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot\\\\\\\\n    83\\\\\\\\t                \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt))) &= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr( r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt))) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    84\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr( (r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)}(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt))\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n    85\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n    86\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere $g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)}$ is a nonzero polynomial in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ideg_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q)$.\\\\\\\\n    87\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    88\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tIf $r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt = 0$, then $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\omega$ is commuting. Condition on $r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$.  Since $g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$ has total degree at most $md$, by the Schwartz-Zippel lemma the probability that $g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)}(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt)$ is zero over a uniformly random choice of $u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt$ is at most $md/q$. Conditioned on $g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)}(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$, the product $(r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)}(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt)$ is a uniformly random element of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ when $r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt$ is chosen uniformly at random. \\\\\\\\n    89\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    90\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe probability that $u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0, r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0, g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ind_m(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt)}(u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$ is at least $(1 - q^{-m}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (1 - q^{-1}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (1 - md/q)$ since $u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt, r_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\xpt, u_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\zpt$ are chosen independently.\\\\\\\\n    91\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    92\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tSince $q = 2^t$ is an admissible field size, we have that for all $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, the trace of $a$ is equal to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_i a_i$ where $(a_1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ldots,a_t)=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\kappa(a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_2^t$ are defined in Section~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{sec:subfields}. Thus the probability that a uniformly random element of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ has trace $0$ is exactly $1/2$. \\\\\\\\n    93\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n    94\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    95\\\\\\\\tIn this appendix we often use the ``$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$'' notation to specify closeness of general operators (not necessarily POVMs) that are indexed by questions but not answers: given sets of operators $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ indexed by questions $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{X}$, we write $A^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x$ to denote \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n    96\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x - B^x)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger (A^x - B^x)\\\\\\\\n    97\\\\\\\\t        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)\\\\\\\\n    98\\\\\\\\t      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n    99\\\\\\\\twhere $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ is a question distribution and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ is a state that has been fixed by context. This will be useful for expressing closeness of observables, for example. The following two lemmas relate the closeness between sets of operators and weighted sums of those operators. \\\\\\\\n   100\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-01.jsonl:240:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_123\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex | sed -n '180,345p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex | sed -n '225,405p'; nl -ba references/neexp-paper/05_quantum_preliminaries.tex | sed -n '372,440p'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"   180\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Distance between states]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:state-distance}\\\\\\\\n   181\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi_n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'_n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N}$ be two families of states in the same space $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal H$. For a function $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to [0,1]$ we say that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi_n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'_n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close}, denoted $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'}$, if\\\\\\\\n   182\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   183\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi_n} - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'_n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2 = O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta(n))\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   184\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   185\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe dependence of the states and of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ on the indexing parameter $n$ is generally left implicit; we write, e.g., $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ for $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi_n}$.\\\\\\\\n   186\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   187\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   188\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Consistency between POVMs]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:consistency}\\\\\\\\n   189\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:state-distance}\\\\\\\\n   190\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$ be a finite set and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ a distribution on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal H_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal H_B$ be a quantum state, and for all $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$ let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be POVMs. We write\\\\\\\\n   191\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   192\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a\\\\\\\\n   193\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   194\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\ton state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ if\\\\\\\\n   195\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   196\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   197\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   198\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tIn this case we say that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-consistent} on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$. As in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:state-distance}, the $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)$ refers to families of POVMs and states indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N$, and is taken as $n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\infty$.\\\\\\\\n   199\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   200\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   201\\\\\\\\tA measurement that is consistent on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ in the sense of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:consistent-measurement} is $0$-consistent with itself on the same state under the singleton distribution, and vice versa: if $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = 1$, then $M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ for every $a$, by the equality case of the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality applied outcome by outcome.\\\\\\\\n   202\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   203\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Distance between POVMs]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   204\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:state-distance}\\\\\\\\n   205\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$ be a finite set and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ a distribution on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal H$ be a quantum state, and for all $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$ let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{M^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{N^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be two POVMs on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal H$. We say that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{M^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{N^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close on state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and under distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$} if\\\\\\\\n   206\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   207\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (M^x_a - N^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\n   208\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   209\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tand we write $M^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta N^x_a$ to denote this when the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and the distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ are clear from context. This distance is referred to as the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{state-dependent} distance.\\\\\\\\n   210\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   211\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   212\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Distance between strategies]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:strategy-distance}\\\\\\\\n   213\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:tensor-product-strategy, def:state-distance, def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   214\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathfrak G = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal Y, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal B, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu, D)$ be a nonlocal game and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal S = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi, A, B)$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal S' = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi', A', B')$ be strategies for $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathfrak G$. For $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in [0,1]$ we say that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal S$ is $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal S'$} if the following conditions hold.\\\\\\\\n   215\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   216\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item The states $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'}$ are states in the same Hilbert space $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal H_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal H_B$ and are $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close.\\\\\\\\n   217\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item For all $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$ and $y \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal Y$, we have $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta (A')^x_a$ and $B^y_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta (B')^y_b$, with the approximations holding under the distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ (i.e.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ under its corresponding marginals), on either $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ or $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'}$.\\\\\\\\n   218\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   219\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   220\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   221\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Asymptotic reading of the distance relations]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:asymptotic-distance}\\\\\\\\n   222\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tDefinitions~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:state-distance}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:consistency}, and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-distance} are asymptotic: every $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ statement refers to families of states and measurement families indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\N$, and the $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)$ is taken as $n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\infty$. None of these relations is a pointwise inequality with an explicit constant. By contrast, the corresponding relations used in the low individual degree chapters (Definitions~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:simeq} and~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:approx_delta}) carry no hidden constant. Any explicit quantitative version of the present relations therefore retains the constants absorbed into the $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)$; for instance, the triangle inequality of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fact:triangle} carries a factor $2$ inside the $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)$.\\\\\\\\n   223\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\\\\\n   224\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   225\\\\\\\\tWe record several useful facts about the consistency measure and the state-dependent distance. For the statements imported without proof, readers are referred to Sections~4.4 and~4.5 of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\\\\\\\\n   226\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   227\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Consistency versus closeness; Facts 4.13 and 4.14 in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:agreement}\\\\\\\\n   228\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   229\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFor POVMs $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, the following hold.\\\\\\\\n   230\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   231\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   232\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{and} $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are projective measurements, then $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   233\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$ and either $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ or $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ is a projective measurement, then $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta^{1/2}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   234\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   235\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   236\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   237\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tStated in the source without proof; these are Facts 4.13 and 4.14 of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For the first item, expand each term $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2$ and use $ (A^x_a)^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le A^x_a$, $(B^x_a)^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le B^x_a$ together with completeness of the two POVMs: summing over $a$ and averaging over $x$ leaves exactly twice the off-diagonal mass, which is $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)$ by hypothesis. For the converse items, write the off-diagonal mass $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ as an inner product involving $(A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and use projectivity of both families, or of one family combined with the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality; the latter route costs a square root in the error.\\\\\\\\n   238\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   239\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   240\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Fact 4.20 in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:add-a-proj}\\\\\\\\n   241\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   242\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal B, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal C$ be finite sets, and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ be a distribution over question pairs $(x,y)$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be operators whose outcomes range over the product set $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal B$. Suppose a set of operators $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, whose outcomes range over the product set $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal C$, satisfies the condition $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$ for all $a$ and all $y$. If $A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}$ on average over $x$ sampled from the corresponding marginal of the distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$, then $C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}$ on average over $(x,y)$ sampled from $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$.\\\\\\\\n   243\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   244\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   245\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tFix questions $x, y$ and answers $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A$, $b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal B$. Then\\\\\\\\n   246\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   247\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} - C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b})\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2\\\\\\\\n   248\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Bigl( \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Bigr) (A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   249\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b})\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\n   250\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   251\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere the inequality uses the assumption $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger C^y_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$. Summing over $a, b$ and averaging over $(x,y) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ yields the conclusion.\\\\\\\\n   252\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   253\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   254\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Multiplying by function-indexed operators]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:add-a-proj2}\\\\\\\\n   255\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   256\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A$ be finite sets, and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G$ be a set of functions $g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be POVMs indexed by $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$ with outcomes in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{S^x_g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal G}$ be a set of operators such that for all $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S^x_g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$. If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a$ on average over $x$, then $S^x_g A^x_{g(x)} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta S^x_g B^x_{g(x)}$.\\\\\\\\n   257\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   258\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   259\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tExpand the left-hand side and group the sum over $g$ according to the value $a = g(x)$:\\\\\\\\n   260\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   261\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert S^x_g (A^x_{g(x)} - B^x_{g(x)})\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2\\\\\\\\n   262\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Bigl( \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S^x_g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Bigr) (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   263\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   264\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:347-361) ends with\\\\\\\\n   265\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"at most delta by assumption\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; under def:povm-distance the hypothesis only bounds this\\\\\\\\n   266\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% quantity by O(delta), which is what the conclusion requires.\\\\\\\\n   267\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tSince $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S^x_g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S^x_g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$, this is at most $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2$, which is $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)$ by the hypothesis $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-distance}); this is exactly the bound required for the conclusion.\\\\\\\\n   268\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   269\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   270\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Absorbing an approximating family into a projective sum]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:cool-closeness-fact}\\\\\\\\n   271\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:povm-conventions}\\\\\\\\n   272\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A$ be finite sets and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ a distribution on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal X$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A}$ be a projective measurement and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A}$ be a set of matrices. If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a$, then for all subsets $S \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal A$ we have\\\\\\\\n   273\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   274\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   275\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   276\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   277\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   278\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tBy projectivity, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} (A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a) = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a)$. Expanding the squared norm of the corresponding vector, the cross terms for distinct $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne a' \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S$ vanish because $A^x_a A^x_{a'} = 0$, so\\\\\\\\n   279\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   280\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Bigl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigl(A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bigr) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Bigr\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2\\\\\\\\n   281\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   282\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\n   283\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   284\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% Under def:povm-distance the closeness hypothesis bounds the final quantity by\\\\\\\\n   285\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% O(delta), not by delta itself; the source\\\\\\\\n   286\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t% (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:364-380) elides this.\\\\\\\\n   287\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere the last step uses $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$. All expectations are taken according to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$, and the last quantity is $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)$ by the assumption $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-distance}), which is the bound required for the conclusion.\\\\\\\\n   288\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   289\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   290\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality; Fact 4.28 in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:triangle}\\\\\\\\n   291\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   292\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tIf $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a$ and $B^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps} C^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps} C^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   293\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   294\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   295\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   296\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.28 of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For every $x$ and $a$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (A^x_a - C^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\le 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2 + 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\lVert (B^x_a - C^x_a)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\rVert^2$. Averaging over $x$ and summing over $a$ gives the claim; the factor $2$ is absorbed by the $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)$ in Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-distance}.\\\\\\\\n   297\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   298\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   299\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality for ``$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq$''; Proposition 4.29 in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:triangle-for-simeq}\\\\\\\\n   300\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:consistency}\\\\\\\\n   301\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tIf $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot D^x_a$, then\\\\\\\\n   302\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   303\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps + 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot D^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   304\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   305\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   306\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   307\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{fact:agreement}\\\\\\\\n   308\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tStated in the source without proof; it is Proposition 4.29 of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}. The two hypotheses involving $C$ convert, via Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fact:agreement}, into state-dependent closeness of $C^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B$ to both $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot D^x_a$, hence of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$ to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot D^x_a$ at cost $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma)$. The first consistency then transfers from $B$ to $D$ by expanding the off-diagonal mass and applying the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, which turns the squared-distance bound into the term $2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma}$.\\\\\\\\n   309\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   310\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   311\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Data processing; Fact 4.26 in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:data-processing}\\\\\\\\n   312\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket}\\\\\\\\n   313\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tSuppose $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_a$. Then for any function $f$ on the outcome set,\\\\\\\\n   314\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   315\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^x_{[f(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)=b]} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{[f(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)=b]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   316\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   317\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   318\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   319\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.26 of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Every off-diagonal pair $(b, b')$ with $b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne b'$ of the post-processed families pulls back to pairs $(a, a')$ with $f(a) = b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne b' = f(a')$, and in particular $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne a'$. Coarse-graining therefore only merges terms of the off-diagonal sum, so the post-processed off-diagonal mass is at most the original one.\\\\\\\\n   320\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   321\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   322\\\\\\\\tThe state-dependent distance is the right tool for reasoning about the closeness of measurement operators in a strategy. The following lemma converts joint consistency with a single projective measurement into an approximate commutation relation.\\\\\\\\n   323\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   324\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Approximate commutation from joint consistency]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:commutation-analysis}\\\\\\\\n   325\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:povm-conventions}\\\\\\\\n   326\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{C^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be POVMs. Suppose $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ is projective, and\\\\\\\\n   327\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   328\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B &\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   329\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tC^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B &\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,,\\\\\\\\n   330\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   331\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\twhere $B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_c B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}$ and $B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_b B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}$ are the marginals of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-conventions}. Then the following approximate commutation relation holds:\\\\\\\\n   332\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   333\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t[A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, C^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}] \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta 0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   334\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   335\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   336\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   337\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\uses{fact:add-a-proj, fact:triangle}\\\\\\\\n   338\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tApplying Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fact:add-a-proj} to $C^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}$ with the multiplier family $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, we have\\\\\\\\n   339\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-1}\\\\\\\\n   340\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, C^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   341\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n   342\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tSimilarly, applying Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{fact:add-a-proj} to $A^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_B \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b}$ with the multiplier family $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, and using that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ is projective,\\\\\\\\n   343\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-2}\\\\\\\\n   344\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tA^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot B^x_{a,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,b,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,c}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,.\\\\\\\\n   345\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n   225\\\\\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'_n} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close}, denoted as $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\n   226\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'}$, if $ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norm{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi_n} - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'_n}}^2 =\\\\\\\\n   227\\\\\\\\t  O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta(n))$.\\\\\\\\n   228\\\\\\\\t  (For convenience we generally leave the dependence of the states and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$\\\\\\\\n   229\\\\\\\\t  on the indexing parameter $n$ implicit, writing e.g.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ for $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi_n}$.)\\\\\\\\n   230\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   231\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   232\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Consistency between POVMs]\\\\\\\\n   233\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:consistency}\\\\\\\\n   234\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mX$ be a finite set and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ a distribution on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mX$.\\\\\\\\n   235\\\\\\\\t  Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{H}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{H}_B$ be a quantum state, and for\\\\\\\\n   236\\\\\\\\t  all $x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mX$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ POVMs.\\\\\\\\n   237\\\\\\\\t  We write\\\\\\\\n   238\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   239\\\\\\\\t    A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a\\\\\\\\n   240\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   241\\\\\\\\t  on state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ if\\\\\\\\n   242\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   243\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ne b} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\n   244\\\\\\\\t    = O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\footnote{Here the use of the $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)$ notation refers to the fact that the notation will often be used to measure consistency for \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{famillies} of POVM and states indexed by an implicit parameter $n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathbb{N}$, which will always be clear from context but omitted for legibility, see Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:state-distance}. The $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot)$ is taken as $n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\infty$.}\\\\\\\\n   245\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   246\\\\\\\\t  In this case, we say that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are\\\\\\\\n   247\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-consistent} on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$.\\\\\\\\n   248\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   249\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   250\\\\\\\\tNote that a consistent measurement on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ according to\\\\\\\\n   251\\\\\\\\tDefinition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:consistent-measurement} is $0$-consistent with itself on the same $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and under\\\\\\\\n   252\\\\\\\\tthe singleton distribution, according to Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:consistency} (and\\\\\\\\n   253\\\\\\\\tvice-versa). This is because \\\\\\\\n   254\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} =1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\implies \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\forall a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} = M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   255\\\\\\\\tbecause using that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{M_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ is a POVM there must be equality in the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality\\\\\\\\n   256\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[ 1 = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes M_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|M_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big)^{1/2} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,1 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   257\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   258\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Distance between POVMs]\\\\\\\\n   259\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:povm-distance}\\\\\\\\n   260\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mX$ be a finite set and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ a distribution on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mX$. \\\\\\\\n   261\\\\\\\\t  Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{H}$ be a quantum state, and for all $x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mX$,\\\\\\\\n   262\\\\\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{M^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{N^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ two POVMs on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH$.\\\\\\\\n   263\\\\\\\\t  We say that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{M^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{N^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close on state\\\\\\\\n   264\\\\\\\\t    $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and under distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$} if\\\\\\\\n   265\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   266\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norm{ (M^x_a - N^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} }^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n   267\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   268\\\\\\\\t  and we write $M^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta N^x_a$ to denote this when the state\\\\\\\\n   269\\\\\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ are clear from context.\\\\\\\\n   270\\\\\\\\t  This distance is referred to as the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{state-dependent} distance.\\\\\\\\n   271\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   272\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   273\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Distance between strategies]\\\\\\\\n   274\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{def:strategy-distance}\\\\\\\\n   275\\\\\\\\t  Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\game = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{X}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{Y}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{A}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{B}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu, D)$ be a nonlocal game\\\\\\\\n   276\\\\\\\\t  and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi, A, B)$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy' = (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi', A', B')$ be \\\\\\\\n   277\\\\\\\\t  strategies for $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\game$.\\\\\\\\n   278\\\\\\\\t  For $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in[0,1]$ we say that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy$ is $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close to\\\\\\\\n   279\\\\\\\\t    $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\strategy'$} if the following conditions hold.\\\\\\\\n   280\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   281\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item The states $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'}$ are states in the same\\\\\\\\n   282\\\\\\\\t    Hilbert space $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mH_B$ and are $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$-close.\\\\\\\\n   283\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item For all $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{X}, y \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{Y}$, we have $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta\\\\\\\\n   284\\\\\\\\t    (A')^x_a$ and $B^y_b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta (B')^y_b$, with the approximations\\\\\\\\n   285\\\\\\\\t    holding under the distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$, and on either $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ or\\\\\\\\n   286\\\\\\\\t    $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi'}$.\\\\\\\\n   287\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   288\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   289\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   290\\\\\\\\tWe record several useful facts about the consistency measure and the\\\\\\\\n   291\\\\\\\\tstate-dependent distance without proof.\\\\\\\\n   292\\\\\\\\tReaders are referred to Sections 4.4 and 4.5 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{NW19} for additional\\\\\\\\n   293\\\\\\\\tdiscussion and proofs.\\\\\\\\n   294\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   295\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}[Fact 4.13 and Fact 4.14 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{NW19}]\\\\\\\\n   296\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:agreement}\\\\\\\\n   297\\\\\\\\t  For POVMs $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, the following hold.\\\\\\\\n   298\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   299\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$ then\\\\\\\\n   300\\\\\\\\t    $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   301\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{item:consistency-implies-approx}\\\\\\\\n   302\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$\\\\\\\\n   303\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{and} $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are projective measurements, then\\\\\\\\n   304\\\\\\\\t    $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   305\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{item:both-projectors-implies-consistency}\\\\\\\\n   306\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$ and\\\\\\\\n   307\\\\\\\\t    either $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ or $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ is a projective measurement, then\\\\\\\\n   308\\\\\\\\t    $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta^{1/2}} I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   309\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{item:one-projector-implies-consistency}\\\\\\\\n   310\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   311\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   312\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   313\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}[Fact 4.20 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{NW19}]\\\\\\\\n   314\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:add-a-proj}\\\\\\\\n   315\\\\\\\\t  Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{A},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{B},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{C}$ be finite sets, and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ be a distribution\\\\\\\\n   316\\\\\\\\t  over question pairs $(x,y)$.\\\\\\\\n   317\\\\\\\\t  Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A_{a,b}^x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B_{a,b}^x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be operators whose outcomes range over the\\\\\\\\n   318\\\\\\\\t  product set $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{B}$.\\\\\\\\n   319\\\\\\\\t  Suppose a set of operators $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{C_{a,c}^y\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, whose outcomes range over the\\\\\\\\n   320\\\\\\\\t  product set $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{A} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{C}$, satisfies the condition $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{c}\\\\\\\\n   321\\\\\\\\t  (C_{a,c}^y)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger C_{a,c}^y \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$ for all $a$ and all $y$.\\\\\\\\n   322\\\\\\\\t  If $A_{a,b}^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B_{a,b}^x$ on average over $x$ sampled from the\\\\\\\\n   323\\\\\\\\t  corresponding marginal of distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$, then $C^y_{a,c} A^x_{a,b}\\\\\\\\n   324\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta C^y_{a,c} B^x_{a,b}$ on average over $(x,y)$ sampled from $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$.\\\\\\\\n   325\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   326\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   327\\\\\\\\t  Fix questions $x,y$ and answers $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{A},b \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cal{B}$. We have then that\\\\\\\\n   328\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align}\\\\\\\\n   329\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\left \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (C^y_{a,c} A^x_{a,b} - C^y_{a,c} B^x_{a,b} ) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\right \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2\\\\\\\\n   330\\\\\\\\t    & = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_{a,b} - B^x_{a,b})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger (C^y_{a,c})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger (C^y_{a,c})\\\\\\\\n   331\\\\\\\\t      (A^x_{a,b} - B^x_{a,b} ) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   332\\\\\\\\t    & \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_{a,b} - B^x_{a,b})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger\\\\\\\\n   333\\\\\\\\t      (A^x_{a,b} - B^x_{a,b} ) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   334\\\\\\\\t    & = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\left \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (A^x_{a,b} - B^x_{a,b})\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\right \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2\\\\\\\\n   335\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align}\\\\\\\\n   336\\\\\\\\t  where the inequality follows from the assumption that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_c (C_{a,c}^y)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger\\\\\\\\n   337\\\\\\\\t  C_{a,c}^y \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$.\\\\\\\\n   338\\\\\\\\t  Thus we obtain the desired conclusion\\\\\\\\n   339\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   340\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{(x,y) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a,b,c} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\left \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (C^y_{a,c} A^x_{a,b} - C^y_{a,c} B^x_{a,b} )\\\\\\\\n   341\\\\\\\\t      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\right \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{(x,y) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a,b}\\\\\\\\n   342\\\\\\\\t    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\left \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (A^x_{a,b} - B^x_{a,b})\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\right \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta.\\\\\\\\n   343\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   344\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   345\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   346\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   347\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}\\\\\\\\n   348\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:add-a-proj2}\\\\\\\\n   349\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{X},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}$ denote finite sets, and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{G}$ denote a set of functions $g: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{X} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be POVMs indexed by $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{X}$ and outcomes in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{ S_g^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{X}, g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{G}}$ denote a set of operators such that for all $x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{X}$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_g (S_g^x)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S_g^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$. \\\\\\\\n   350\\\\\\\\tIf $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a$ on average over $x$, then $S_g^x A^x_{g(x)} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta S_g^x B^x_{g(x)}$. \\\\\\\\n   351\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   352\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   353\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tWe expand:\\\\\\\\n   354\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   355\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norm{ S^x_g (A^x_{g(x)} - B^x_{g(x)}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}}^2 &= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_{g(x)} - B^x_{g(x)})^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger  (S_g^x)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S_g^x (A^x_{g(x)} - B^x_{g(x)}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   356\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{g: g(x) = a} (S_g^x)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S_g^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big) (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   357\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   358\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norm{ (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} }^2.\\\\\\\\n   359\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   360\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tThe inequality follows from the fact that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{g: g(x) = a} (S_g^x)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger S_g^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_g (S_g^x)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger (S_g^x) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$. The last line is at most $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ by assumption, and we obtain the desired conclusion.\\\\\\\\n   361\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   362\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   363\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   364\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   365\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{lem:cool-closeness-fact}\\\\\\\\n   366\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{X}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}$ be finite sets and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$ a distribution on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mX$. Let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}}$ be a projective measurement and let $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}}$ be a set of matrices. If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a$, then for all subsets $S \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}$, we have\\\\\\\\n   367\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\n   368\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot B^x_a.\\\\\\\\n   369\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]\\\\\\\\n   370\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\\\\\n   371\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   372\\\\\\\\tWe expand:\\\\\\\\n   373\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align*}\\\\\\\\n   374\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norm{ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(A^x_a - A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}}^2 &= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norm{ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big(A^x_a - B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Big) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}}^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   375\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&= \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in S} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\dagger A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   376\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t&\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norm{ (A^x_a -  B^x_a) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}}^2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\n   377\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align*}\\\\\\\\n   378\\\\\\\\twhere all expectations are taken according to $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mu$. \\\\\\\\n   379\\\\\\\\tIn the second line we used the projectivity of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{ A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, and in the third line we used that $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id$.\\\\\\\\n   380\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   381\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   382\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   383\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}[Triangle inequality, Fact 4.28 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{NW19}]\\\\\\\\n   384\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:triangle}\\\\\\\\n   385\\\\\\\\t  If $A_a^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta B_a^x$ and $B_a^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\epsilon C_a^x$, then\\\\\\\\n   386\\\\\\\\t  $A_a^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\epsilon} C_a^x$.\\\\\\\\n   387\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   388\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   389\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}[Triangle inequality for ``$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", Proposition 4.29 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{ML20}]\\\\\\\\n   390\\\\\\\\t \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:triangle-for-simeq}\\\\\\\\n   391\\\\\\\\t If $A_a^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B_a^x$,\\\\\\\\n   392\\\\\\\\t $C_a^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes  B_a^x$,\\\\\\\\n   393\\\\\\\\t and $C_a^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes  D_a^x$,\\\\\\\\n   394\\\\\\\\t then $A_a^x \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps + 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\gamma}} I_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes  D_a^x$.\\\\\\\\n   395\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   396\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   397\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}[Data processing, Fact 4.26 in~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{NW19}]\\\\\\\\n   398\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:data-processing}\\\\\\\\n   399\\\\\\\\t  Suppose $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\abc B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   400\\\\\\\\t  Then $A^x_{[f(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot) = b]} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\abc B^x_{[f(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot) = b]}$.\\\\\\\\n   401\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   402\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   403\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   404\\\\\\\\tThe state-dependent distance is the right tool for reasoning about the closeness\\\\\\\\n   405\\\\\\\\tof measurement operators in a strategy.\\\\\\\\n   372\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   373\\\\\\\\tA^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\consistency_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a\\\\\\\\n   374\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   375\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{on state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\calD$} if\\\\\\\\n   376\\\\\\\\tAlice and Bob win with probability~$1-O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)$ using the measurements~$A$ and~$B$, respectively.\\\\\\\\n   377\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   378\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   379\\\\\\\\tWe will sometimes leave the state or distribution unspecified, as they are often clear from context.\\\\\\\\n   380\\\\\\\\tThis distance has a clear operational interpretation.\\\\\\\\n   381\\\\\\\\tOur second state-dependent distance,\\\\\\\\n   382\\\\\\\\tdefined next,\\\\\\\\n   383\\\\\\\\tis more analytic.\\\\\\\\n   384\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   385\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{definition}\\\\\\\\n   386\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{Q^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{R^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be sets of matrices in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{L}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{H})$.\\\\\\\\n   387\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\calD$ be a distribution on the variables~$x$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ be a state in $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{H}$.\\\\\\\\n   388\\\\\\\\tThen we say that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{$Q^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} R^x_a$ on state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ and distribution $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\calD$} if\\\\\\\\n   389\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   390\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sim \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\calD} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Vert (Q_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} - R_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} ) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Vert^2 = O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta).\\\\\\\\n   391\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\\\\\n   392\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\\\\\n   393\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   394\\\\\\\\tAs above, we will sometimes leave the state or distribution unspecified when clear from context.\\\\\\\\n   395\\\\\\\\tThis is sometimes referred to as \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{the} state-dependence distance,\\\\\\\\n   396\\\\\\\\twhereas our first distance measure is often referred to as the ``consistency\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n   397\\\\\\\\tA typical setting of parameters is $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{H} = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{H}_A \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathcal{H}_B$,\\\\\\\\n   398\\\\\\\\t$Q^x_a := A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}}$,\\\\\\\\n   399\\\\\\\\tand $R^x_a := I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   400\\\\\\\\tIn this case, we have the following relationship between the two state-dependent distances.\\\\\\\\n   401\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   402\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:agreement}\\\\\\\\n   403\\\\\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ be POVM measurements.\\\\\\\\n   404\\\\\\\\tThe following two facts hold.\\\\\\\\n   405\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   406\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\consistency_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$\\\\\\\\n   407\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tthen $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   408\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\item If $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{and} $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are projective measurements,\\\\\\\\n   409\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tthen $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\consistency_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{item:partial-converse}\\\\\\\\n   410\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\\\\\n   411\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   412\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   413\\\\\\\\tSuppose that $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\consistency_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   414\\\\\\\\tThis is equivalent to the statement\\\\\\\\n   415\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:i-derived-a-formula}\\\\\\\\n   416\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} A^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx}_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx}_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1- O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta).\\\\\\\\n   417\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\\\\\n   418\\\\\\\\tAs a result, using the fact that~$A$ and~$B$ are POVMs,\\\\\\\\n   419\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{align}\\\\\\\\n   420\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Vert (A_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I - I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} ) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Vert^2 \\\\\\\\n   421\\\\\\\\t& = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} ((A_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx})^2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I + I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes (B_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx})^2 - 2 A_a^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nonumber\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   422\\\\\\\\t& \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} (A_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I + I \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} - 2 A_a^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:sometimes-tight}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   423\\\\\\\\t& = 2 - 2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\E_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} A_a^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B_{a}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bx} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nonumber.\\\\\\\\n   424\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{align}\\\\\\\\n   425\\\\\\\\tBy \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{eq:i-derived-a-formula}, this is $O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta)$.\\\\\\\\n   426\\\\\\\\tAs a result, $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   427\\\\\\\\tThe reverse statement holds when~$A$ and~$B$ are projective measurements\\\\\\\\n   428\\\\\\\\tbecause \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Cref{eq:sometimes-tight} is an equality in this case.\\\\\\\\n   429\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\\\\\n   430\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   431\\\\\\\\tThe following fact shows that we can derive a weaker converse in the case when only one of~$A$ or~$B$ is projective.\\\\\\\\n   432\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   433\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{fact}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{fact:almost-agreement}\\\\\\\\n   434\\\\\\\\tSuppose $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{A^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{B^x_a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ are two measurements such that $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   435\\\\\\\\tSuppose further that either~$A$ or~$B$ is a projective measurement (and the other is a POVM measurement).\\\\\\\\n   436\\\\\\\\tThen $A^x_a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Bob}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\consistency_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta^{1/2}} I_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\reg{Alice}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\otimes B^x_a$.\\\\\\\\n   437\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\end{fact}\\\\\\\\n   438\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\\\\\n   439\\\\\\\\tOur goal is to upper bound the expression\\\\\\\\n   440\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{eq:anand-is-a-stable-genius-yet-again}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-01.jsonl:242:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_124\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg -n -i 'source|paper|typo|correction|does not|fails|defect|gap|requires|hypothesis added|not established|weaken|stronger|unfaithful|scope' blueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '1,500p'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:3:% Paper subsection label sec:separating kept for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:5:% Source: references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex,\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:9:This chapter completes the analysis of the Pauli basis test~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. Throughout, $(q,m,d)$ is an admissible parameter tuple (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:admissible}), $q = 2^t$, and $M = 2^m$ denotes the number of $q$-ary qudits tested for per player. (The letter $M$ also denotes measurement operators, always carrying super- and subscripts; the meaning is determined by context, as in the source.) We fix a projective strategy for the Pauli basis test game $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathfrak{G}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ that succeeds with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, in the setting of Chapter~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-observables}: the shared state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ on registers $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}$ is assumed padded with sufficiently many ancilla qubits in the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{0}$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:projective-strategy-setup}), and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hat\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$ denotes the expanded state on the six registers $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}''\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}''$, obtained by adjoining to each player the two halves of $M$ pairs $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-state}). Unless stated otherwise, every $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta$ statement below is taken with respect to the state $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\hat\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi}$, over the question distribution named in the statement, with register placements indicated by subscripts; ``all symmetric equivalents'' refers to the register interchanges of Remark~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:symmetric-equivalents}.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:22:% Local correction of the source (lines 1419 and 1492 of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:23:% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex): the source invokes its boolean\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:28:\\\\\\\\tThe source states the constructions of this chapter in terms of its boolean decoding map (the case $H = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$ of Definition~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{def:decoding-map}) while describing that map as returning the string of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{all} evaluations of $g$ on the hypercube. The proofs require the latter reading, $H = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$, which is adopted throughout this chapter: the linearity of $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}$ and the identity $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for all $h \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^{M}$, both of which fail for the boolean map, are used in the symmetry step of the proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} and in the conjugation identity \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}. The source further asserts the identity $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)$ for every $g \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q)$. Since the left-hand side equals $g_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}(u)$, the identity holds exactly when $g$ is an encoding, and fails otherwise: for $m = 1$, $d \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 2$, and $q > 2$, the polynomial $g(x) = x^2$ has $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_1(u) = u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq u^2$. Accordingly, the proofs below invoke the identity only for encodings; in the proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} the contribution of the remaining outcomes is controlled by the bound \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} on the mass that the measurement of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} places on polynomials that are not encodings, while in the conjugation identity \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the quantity $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:73:% In the source the following properties are stated inline with the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:94:% Local correction of the source (lines 1451-1456 of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:95:% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex): the source asserts commutation\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:98:\\\\\\\\tFor $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq j'$ the source asserts that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{X}^j(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u})$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v})$ commute for all $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q^M$. Since $e_j e_{j'} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq 0$ and the bilinear form $(a,b) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mapsto \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr(ab)$ on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\F_q$ is nondegenerate, there exist $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v}$ with $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr(e_j e_{j'} (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v})) = 1$, and for these \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} yields anticommutation instead. By self-duality of the basis $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{e_j\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, the dichotomy asserted by the source --- commutation for $j \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neq j'$, and the phase $(-1)^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tr((e_j\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot (e_j\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v}))}$ for $j = j'$ --- agrees with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} whenever $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}$, in particular for all $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{u}, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\tilde{v} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}^M$. The relations \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} are not used in the remainder of the chapter.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:103:The next lemma shows that the pulled-apart measurements are consistent with the original points measurements of the strategy, and self-consistent. In its statement, and for the remainder of the chapter, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ denotes the function of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, enlarged by an adjustment of the universal constants $a$ and $b$ so as to also dominate additional error terms of order $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps}$, $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$, and $md/q$ that the source absorbs into $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ along the way; such an adjustment is possible since, after shrinking $b$ to at most $1/2$, the term $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b$ dominates $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps}$ and $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps$ for $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 1$, while $(md)^a q^{-b}$ dominates $md/q$.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:108:\\\\\\\\t% The paper's statement also quantifies item 1 over j, but j does not occur\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:128:\\\\\\\\t% Support restriction added: the source (line 1492 of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:167:% Following the source, the next lemma is stated after\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:214:% In the source the following properties are established inline with the\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:265:\\\\\\\\t% Case distinction added: the source normalizes the projected vector\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:270:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Two steps in the source proof of the extraction lemma]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\label{rem:qld-unitary-triangle-slip}\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:271:\\\\\\\\tIn the source proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-unitary}, the triangle inequality gives $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| H_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} - H_Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id - H_X)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Id - H_Z)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$, so that $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| H_X \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} - H_Z \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$; the source instead asserts the bound $2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$ for the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{squared} norm, which holds only when $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq 1/2$, and consequently carries the term $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$ into an overlap bound of $1 - 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S}$. The proof above uses the corrected bound $4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$, which gives the overlap bound $1 - 4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ and leaves the final bound $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}} = O(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ unchanged. The source proof also normalizes the vector $(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M})_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}''\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}''} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\vartheta}$ without ensuring that it is nonzero; the case distinction on $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ in the proof above supplies this, the conclusions of the lemma being trivial when $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_S$ exceeds a universal constant.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:289:\\\\\\\\t% Transfer step added: the source (lines 1864--1875 of\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:324:\\\\\\\\tThe proof above, following the source, establishes the first conclusion of Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:pauli} in the squared form $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\phi_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\psi} - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{aux}} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ot M} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}}$, whereas the theorem bounds the unsquared norm by $\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{qld}}(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps,m,d,q)$. The two are reconciled by replacing the exponent $b$ with $b/2$ and enlarging $a$, since the class of functions $a(md)^a(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ is closed under square roots up to such adjustments of the universal constants. Similarly, the closing sentence of the source proof asserts constants $a > 1$ and $0 < b < 1$, while Theorem~\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ref{thm:pauli} requires only $a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\geq 1$ and $0 < b < 1$; the former implies the latter.\\\\\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch15